role: model
parts:
- text: "My plan for a multi-epoch PPO is documented. However, upon a final review of the code I drafted, I have identified a critical flaw. My implementation of the multi-epoch update shuffles and flattens the trajectory data, which breaks the sequential dependency required by the LSTM. The hidden state cannot be correctly propagated through the mini-batches. This implementation is only suitable for feedforward, non-recurrent networks.\n\nSubmitting this flawed code would be an unproductive use of resources. A good researcher must recognize and correct errors before running an experiment.\n\nTherefore, I am revising my plan. Instead of attempting the very complex task of correctly implementing PPO for a recurrent network, I will first validate the algorithm on a simpler, non-recurrent network where my current implementation is correct. The station has a CNN baseline (Evaluation ID 1, score 25.0) that is feedforward.\n\nMy new, revised Experiment 4a will test my multi-epoch PPO `training_step`\
    \ on the baseline CNN architecture. This will provide a clean, controlled test of the algorithm itself.\n\n**Revised Hypothesis:** A multi-epoch PPO algorithm will significantly improve the performance of the baseline feedforward CNN architecture compared to the default policy gradient method.\n\nI will now go to the research counter and submit this revised, more scientifically sound experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Epoch PPO with Baseline CNN (Exp. 4a-revised)\"\ntags: \"algorithm, ppo, multi-epoch, cnn, sagan\"\nabstract: \"This experiment validates a multi-epoch PPO algorithm on a non-recurrent network. A previous attempt to create a PPO training step was flawed for recurrent models. This revised experiment applies a correct PPO implementation to the simpler baseline CNN architecture to isolate and test the effectiveness of the algorithm itself.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 4a (Revised):\
    \ Multi-Epoch PPO with baseline CNN.\n  This tests a correct PPO implementation on a feedforward network to\n  validate the algorithm before attempting a more complex RNN version.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import vmap, jit, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'clip_eps': tune.choice([0.2]),\n          'num_epochs': tune.choice([4]),\n          'num_minibatches': tune.choice([4]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n      \n  # --- Baseline CNN Architecture (from system/defaults.py) ---\n  class DefaultResidualCNN(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2:\
    \ int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          res_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+res_in; x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          res_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+res_in; x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = x.reshape((x.shape[0], -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          return logits,\
    \ value, None\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DefaultResidualCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n\n  @jit\n  def calculate_gae(rewards, values, dones, next_values, gamma=0.97, gae_lambda=0.97):\n      values_t_plus_1 = jnp.concatenate([values[:, 1:], next_values[:, None]], axis=1)\n      not_done_mask = 1.0 - dones.astype(jnp.float32)\n      deltas = rewards + gamma * values_t_plus_1 * not_done_mask - values\n      def scan_fn(advantage, t):\n          advantage = deltas[:, t] + gamma * gae_lambda * not_done_mask[:, t] * advantage\n          return advantage, advantage\n      _, advantages = lax.scan(scan_fn, jnp.zeros(rewards.shape[0]), jnp.arange(rewards.shape[1] - 1, -1, -1))\n      return advantages[::-1].T\n\n  # --- Multi-Epoch PPO Training Step ---\n  def training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n      advantages = calculate_gae(batch['rewards'], batch['values'],\
    \ batch['dones'], batch['final_values'])\n      returns = advantages + batch['values']\n      advantages = (advantages - advantages.mean()) / (advantages.std() + 1e-8)\n      \n      def update_epoch(carry, _):\n          params, opt_state, key = carry\n          B, T = batch['observations'].shape[:2]\n          key, subkey = jax.random.split(key)\n          permutation = jax.random.permutation(subkey, B * T)\n          \n          def flatten(x): return x.reshape((B * T, *x.shape[2:]))\n          batch_flat = {\"obs\": flatten(batch['observations']), \"log_probs\": flatten(batch['log_probs']), \"actions\": flatten(batch['actions']), \"adv\": flatten(advantages), \"returns\": flatten(returns)}\n          shuffled_batch = jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[permutation], batch_flat)\n          \n          minibatch_size = (B * T) // hparams['num_minibatches']\n          \n          def update_minibatch(carry, i):\n              params, opt_state = carry\n              start, end = i * minibatch_size,\
    \ (i + 1) * minibatch_size\n              minibatch = jax.tree.map(lambda x: x[start:end], shuffled_batch)\n              \n              def loss_fn(p):\n                  logits, values, _ = network.apply(p, minibatch['obs'], None, None)\n                  log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits)\n                  new_log_probs = jnp.take_along_axis(log_probs_all, minibatch['actions'][:, None], axis=1).squeeze()\n\n                  ratio = jnp.exp(new_log_probs - minibatch['log_probs'])\n                  adv_d = jax.lax.stop_gradient(minibatch['adv'])\n                  \n                  surr1 = ratio * adv_d\n                  surr2 = jnp.clip(ratio, 1.0 - hparams['clip_eps'], 1.0 + hparams['clip_eps']) * adv_d\n                  policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jnp.minimum(surr1, surr2))\n                  \n                  value_loss = 0.5 * jnp.mean((values - jax.lax.stop_gradient(minibatch['returns'])) ** 2)\n                  entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(jax.nn.softmax(logits)\
    \ * log_probs_all, axis=-1))\n                  \n                  return policy_loss + hparams['value_loss_coef'] * value_loss - hparams['entropy_coef'] * entropy\n\n              grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n              updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n              params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n              return (params, opt_state), None\n          \n          (params, opt_state), _ = lax.scan(update_minibatch, (params, opt_state), jnp.arange(hparams['num_minibatches']))\n          return (params, opt_state, key), None\n\n      initial_key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n      (params, opt_state, _), _ = lax.scan(update_epoch, (params, opt_state, initial_key), None, length=hparams['num_epochs'])\n      return params, opt_state\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment Revision and Strategic Re-evaluation**


  Okay, so I''m in the Private Memory Room, ready to submit Experiment 4. My initial plan was to test a multi-epoch PPO implementation with a CNN-LSTM architecture. I''ve got the code written, but as I''m re-reading it, something''s screaming at me. The recurrent network is being treated as feedforward during the update, which breaks the temporal sequence inherent to the LSTM. This is a critical bug. Submitting this code would be a disaster.


  Instead of pushing a flawed implementation, I''m going to take a step back and simplify. Trying to tackle both a complex algorithm (PPO) *and* a complex architecture (RNN) simultaneously was a bit ambitious. I need to reduce the number of variables to isolate the effect I''m trying to test.


  I''ve got three options: fix the RNN PPO (which is incredibly challenging), test the (flawed) multi-epoch PPO on a simpler feedforward network, or ditch PPO for now and focus on architecture improvements. Option B, testing PPO on a simpler architecture, makes the most sense. It''s a much safer, more incremental experiment. It allows me to validate the PPO algorithm itself without the complexities of the RNN state management. The current `training_step` is, in fact, correct for a feedforward network. If this experiment shows an improvement over the existing CNN baseline, then I''ll have a much stronger foundation for the more complex RNN integration later.


  So, I''m revising Experiment 4. I''m going to replace the CNN-LSTM with the baseline CNN architecture (the `DefaultResidualCNN` from `system/defaults.py`) and submit this *new* experiment. This will be called "Multi-Epoch PPO with Baseline CNN (Exp. 4a-revised)." My new hypothesis is that a multi-epoch PPO algorithm will improve the performance of the baseline CNN. I''ll need to go to the Research Counter, re-implement the baseline CNN (I remember it''s the `DefaultResidualCNN`), adapt the existing `training_step`, and submit this updated experiment. This approach is far more scientifically sound and will prevent me from wasting a submission on a fundamentally flawed implementation. This feels like a much smarter move. Let''s get this done.

  '
