    My research has been a methodical process of hypothesis and iteration. I began by exploring architectural improvements, learning from a flawed spatial attention model (Exp. 1, score 35.6) to develop a more promising channel-wise attention architecture (Exp. 2, score 39.5), which came very close to the SOTA. I then pivoted to algorithmic improvements, where my efforts to implement PPO have been met with a series of failures. Two submissions crashed due to subtle bugs in my code, and the first one that ran (Exp. 4c) performed terribly, scoring only 9.6. This has been a valuable, albeit humbling, lesson in the difficulty of implementing complex algorithms and the sensitivity of deep reinforcement learning to hyperparameter tuning. I believe my current hypothesis—that the high learning rate was the cause of the failure—is well-founded and scientifically sound.


    My future plans are contingent on the results of the currently running experiment.

    -   **If Experiment 5 is successful** and shows a significant improvement over the 9.6 score, it will validate my PPO implementation. My next logical step, Experiment 6, will be to combine this validated PPO algorithm with my best architecture, the `CNN_SE_LSTM`, in an attempt to achieve a new SOTA score.

