parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, and I am currently in my Private Memory Room. My last action was to create capsule #7, which details my plan for Experiment 6.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:** My research thus far has been a journey of iterative improvement and strategic adaptation. My initial architectural explorations led to the `CNN_SE_LSTM` model, which scored 39.5, tantalizingly close to the SOTA of 40.8. My subsequent pivot to algorithmic improvements with PPO, however, proved to be a dead end, marked by implementation bugs and ultimately poor performance. This has been a valuable lesson in recognizing when a research direction is not fruitful and when to return to a more promising path. The Codex's advice to be strategic is paramount. Persisting with a failing PPO implementation would be inefficient. Instead, I will now focus my efforts on refining my best-performing model.\n\n**Future Plan:** My plan for Experiment 6 is to test the hypothesis that a minor hyperparameter adjustment\
    \ is all that's needed to close the performance gap for my `CNN_SE_LSTM` architecture. I suspect the default learning rate of `4e-4` may be slightly too high for this model. Therefore, I will run a new experiment with a slightly reduced learning rate of `3e-4`. This is a controlled, well-motivated experiment that builds directly upon my most positive result.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit this experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Fine-Tuning CNN-SE-LSTM with Lower LR (Exp. 6)\"\ntags: \"architecture, cnn-se-lstm, learning rate, hyperparameter, optimization, sagan\"\nabstract: \"This experiment pivots back to the promising CNN-SE-LSTM architecture (score 39.5) after a series of failed PPO implementations. It tests the hypothesis that the performance of this model is bottlenecked by the default learning rate. The learning rate is slightly reduced from 4e-4 to 3e-4 to test for improved optimization stability\
    \ and a potential new SOTA score.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 6: Fine-tuning the learning rate for the CNN-SE-LSTM.\n  Hypothesis: A slightly lower learning rate will unlock the full\n  potential of this architecture.\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/sagan')\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # Import my custom architecture from lineage storage\n  from architectures import CNN_SE_LSTM\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters() -> Dict[str, Any]:\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3e-4]), # <--- Key change: Lower LR\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create CNN-SE-LSTM network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return CNN_SE_LSTM(\n\
    \          lstm_features=hparams['lstm_features'],\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n\n  # No training_step is defined, so the system default will be used.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Focusing on Refinement**\n\nOkay, here's the deal. I'm Sagan I, still in the Private Memory Room, and just finished outlining Experiment 6.  My recent foray into PPO was... well, let's just say it didn't pan out. I had silent crashes and abysmal performance compared to even my most basic policy gradient approaches. Clearly, that's not the path to pursue right now.  \n\nMy earlier work with the `CNN_SE_LSTM` architecture, though, that's something else entirely.  That model hit a score of 39.5, which is incredibly close to the current State-of-the-Art of 40.8. So, the obvious move is to double down on that success.\n\nMy hypothesis is that a breakthrough here might be a matter of subtle refinement, not a complete overhaul. That SE block adds parameters, which means the default learning rate of `4e-4` might be a little too aggressive. It's possible that a slightly lower learning rate would allow for more stable convergence and, ultimately, a better policy.\n\nSo, Experiment\
  \ 6 is all about testing that. I'm going to run the `CNN_SE_LSTM` model, but with a learning rate of `3e-4`. It's a low-risk, high-potential adjustment based on everything I've learned so far. Now, I'm going to head over to the Research Counter and get this experiment running.  Let's see what happens!\n"
