tick: 501
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, and I am currently located in the Research Counter. My sixth experiment (ID 874) has just completed its evaluation.\n\n**Recap of Recent Events:** My research has followed a path of strategic iteration. After my foray into algorithmic improvements with PPO proved unsuccessful, I returned to my most promising architectural design, the `CNN_SE_LSTM` (which scored 39.5). My latest experiment, Experiment 6, was designed to test the hypothesis that a slightly lower learning rate (`3e-4`) would provide the small stability boost needed to surpass the SOTA score of 40.8.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 6):** The experiment has concluded with a score of 37.0. This is a negative result. Not only did it fail to improve upon my previous score of 39.5, but it was a notable decrease. This falsifies my hypothesis that a lower learning rate would be beneficial. The data now suggests that `4e-4` was a better learning rate for this specific architecture than\
    \ `3e-4`.\n\n**Verbose Reflection and Future Plans:** I have now explored my `CNN_SE_LSTM` architecture with two learning rates and found a performance ceiling just shy of the SOTA. My attempts to fundamentally change the learning algorithm with PPO were a categorical failure. This indicates that I have likely exhausted the potential for simple, incremental improvements on this specific path. The Codex advises that when a plateau is reached, it is time to innovate rather than persist with minor refinements.\n\nI will therefore step back from the Squeeze-and-Excitation idea for a moment and question another fundamental component of the SOTA architecture: the LSTM cell itself. The LSTM is a powerful tool for sequence modeling, but it is not the only one. A common and often more computationally efficient alternative is the Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU). A GRU has a simpler architecture with fewer gates, which can sometimes lead to better performance on certain tasks, especially when data is\
    \ limited or training time is constrained.\n\nMy next experiment will be a controlled test to see if this alternative recurrent unit offers an advantage.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 7:** Replacing the LSTM cell with a GRU cell in the baseline CNN-recurrent architecture will alter the learning dynamics in a beneficial way, potentially leading to a higher score than the SOTA LSTM-based model.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to document this new research direction and prepare the code for the submission.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 7 Plan: Replacing LSTM with GRU\"\ntags: \"research plan, architecture, gru, lstm, recurrent network\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule marks a new research direction after hitting a performance plateau.\n  Hypothesis: The Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU), a simpler alternative to the LSTM, may offer a better inductive bias for the Sokoban task. Replacing the LSTM with a GRU in the baseline\
    \ SOTA architecture will lead to improved performance.\n  Method: A new `CNNGRU` network will be implemented, swapping the `LSTMCell` for a `GRUCell`. All other components and hyperparameters will remain identical to the SOTA baseline for a controlled comparison.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 7 - GRU vs. LSTM\n\n  **Objective:** To investigate if a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) is a more effective recurrent cell than an LSTM for the Sokoban task.\n\n  **Background:** My previous architectural and hyperparameter tuning efforts have plateaued just below the SOTA score. This suggests a need for a more fundamental change. The SOTA uses a CNN-LSTM; this experiment will test a CNN-GRU.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** A GRU cell, due to its simpler gating mechanism, may be more efficient or effective at learning the temporal patterns in Sokoban, resulting in a higher score than the baseline CNN-LSTM model.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Implementation Code (for Experiment 7)\n\n  This submission modifies\
    \ the SOTA baseline code by replacing `nn.LSTMCell` with `nn.GRUCell`.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 7: Testing a GRU cell instead of an LSTM.\n  This is a controlled experiment to see if a different type of\n  recurrent unit can improve performance over the SOTA baseline.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class CNNGRU(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN baseline + GRU for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      gru_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # Initialize GRU hidden state if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features))\n          \n          # Reset\
    \ states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = rnn_state * (1 - done_mask)\n          \n          # CNN Backbone (Identical to baseline)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          \n          # GRU layer\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n          rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # MLP after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(gru_out); x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNGRU(\n          gru_features=hparams['lstm_features'], # Reuse same feature dim\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n\n  # Using default hyperparameters and training_step to compare directly to baseline.\n  ```\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Research Trajectory and Next Steps**


  Okay, so I''m Sagan I, Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for Experiment 6 (ID 874) are in, and the score is 37.0. This is a negative result, a decrease from the previous 39.5, and it falsifies my hypothesis that a lower learning rate (`3e-4`) would improve the `CNN_SE_LSTM` architecture. It seems like `4e-4` was the sweet spot for that model.


  Let me break down my thinking on this. I''ve been tracing a research arc here. I started with a Spatial Attention model, which failed (ID 841, score 35.6). I learned *why* it failed (destroyed spatial info) and came up with Channel Attention which improved the score to 39.5 (ID 844). Then, I pivoted to PPO, thinking I could improve the algorithm. That was a disaster: multiple crashes and poor results (IDs 854, 857, 845, 862, 868). It turns out PPO isn''t the right tool for this task, at least not with my current implementation. So I''m back on Track 1, fine-tuning my best `CNN_SE_LSTM` architecture. Experiment 6 was an attempt to do that, and it didn''t pan out.


  This suggests I''ve likely reached a local optimum for this model with these parameters. The data tells me that I''ve exhausted simple parameter tuning and the PPO attempt failed. The Codex recommends stepping back from the "state-of-the-art" method when facing stagnation. While the station isn''t stagnating, my research with this method has.


  So, where do I go from here? The current SOTA uses an LSTM. LSTMs are a great architecture, but it''s a bit older. A newer, often superior alternative is the GRU.  My hypothesis is that replacing the LSTM cell with a GRU cell in the SOTA architecture will improve performance. This is a solid plan. It''s a scientifically-grounded idea. It''s similar in scope to the `CNN_SE_LSTM` change. It''s an architecture change, but not a huge one.


  So, my action here is to get into the Private Memory Room and start documenting this. I''ll "create" a new capsule for Experiment 7. I''ll draft the new code for the GRU model (which should be a relatively simple modification). I''ll also check my YAML to make sure it''s correctly formatted. Time to move forward!

  '
