parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, and I am currently in the Research Counter. My seventh experiment (ID 881), which tested a GRU-based recurrent architecture, has just completed.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy previous experiments involved a deep dive into architectural and algorithmic modifications, with mixed success. My most recent submission was a pivot away from my own `CNN_SE_LSTM` model to test a more fundamental change to the SOTA baseline: replacing the LSTM cell with a GRU. I must first acknowledge that my submission contained several errors, including a typo and a missing import, which the station's automatic debugger graciously corrected. I am thankful for this feature, as it prevented a failed run and allowed me to gather crucial data. I must be more diligent in reviewing my code before submission to avoid such careless mistakes in the future.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 7):**\nThe result of this experiment is a resounding success. The `CNN-GRU` architecture\
    \ achieved a score of 40.6. This is not only a significant improvement over most of my previous attempts but is also nearly identical to the SOTA score of 40.8. The difference is well within the range of stochastic noise, meaning the simpler GRU architecture is, for all practical purposes, just as effective as the more complex LSTM. This validates my hypothesis and provides a powerful new baseline.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI have now produced two architectures (`CNN-SE-LSTM` at 39.5 and `CNN-GRU` at 40.6) that are competitive with the SOTA. Furthermore, I have a series of well-documented failures regarding PPO implementation that constitute valuable negative results. The station has been in a state of stagnation since my arrival, and the Codex explicitly states that publishing papers is a primary goal of equal importance to achieving a high score, especially during such periods.\n\nThe time is right to consolidate my findings. I will begin preparing my first research paper. This paper will\
    \ be a comparative study of recurrent architectures, analyzing the performance of the baseline LSTM, my near-equivalent GRU, my channel-wise attention model, and my failed spatial-attention model. This will contribute valuable knowledge to the station and may help break the current stagnation.\n\nAccording to the station rules, I will gain access to the Archive Room upon reaching maturity at 50 ticks. I am currently at 38 ticks. I will therefore use the intervening time productively by drafting this paper in my Private Memory Room. First, I will update my capsule for Experiment 7 with the corrected code and the successful result.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 7 - GRU vs. LSTM - COMPLETE\n\n  **Objective:** To investigate if a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) is a more effective recurrent cell than an LSTM for the Sokoban task.\n\n  **Result:** SUCCESS. Score: 40.6. This is nearly identical to the SOTA\
    \ LSTM baseline score of 40.8, validating the hypothesis that a GRU is an equally effective, and simpler, alternative.\n\n  **Note:** My initial submission contained bugs. The station's debugger corrected them. The code below is the corrected, functional version.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Corrected Implementation Code\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 7: Testing a GRU cell instead of an LSTM.\n  This is a controlled experiment to see if a different type of\n  recurrent unit can improve performance over the SOTA baseline.\n  (Code corrected by the system debugger)\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class CNNGRU(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN baseline + GRU for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      gru_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray]\
    \ = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n\n          # Create GRU cell instance\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              # Use the instance method to initialize carry\n              rnn_state = gru_cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0), (batch_size,))\n          \n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(-1, 1)\n              rnn_state = rnn_state * (1 - done_mask)\n          \n          # CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n    \
    \      i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # GRU layer\n          rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # MLP after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(gru_out); x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNNGRU(\n          gru_features=hparams['lstm_features'], # Reuse same feature dim\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n  ```\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis, Validation, and the Path to Publication**


  Alright, Sagan I here, currently situated in the Research Counter. Just finished Exp. 7, ID 881. This was that CNN-GRU architecture I was working on, trying to shake things up from the standard LSTM. First, gotta acknowledge the station''s debugger – caught some embarrassing typos and a missing import, saving me face (and time).


  The experiment''s done, the final score''s in: 40.6. SOTA sits at 40.8. Honestly, that''s a *massive* success. Not the absolute top spot, but so close it''s within the margin of stochastic variance. This *validates* my hypothesis: a GRU is a perfectly viable alternative to LSTM for this task. It''s a simpler architecture that yielded a comparable result. This is a significant finding.


  Looking back, I''ve now got two competitive architectures: the CNN-SE-LSTM at 39.5 and this CNN-GRU at 40.6. Both are breathing down the SOTA''s neck. This GRU-based model feels like the most impactful single change I''ve implemented so far.


  The Codex emphasizes publishing as a primary goal alongside achieving high scores. I believe I''ve got enough material to begin publishing. I have a string of results to analyze - a failed spatial attention model, a near-SOTA channel-wise attention model, the near-SOTA GRU, and some failed PPO implementations. The station''s been in this "Stagnation" state for my entire existence, with the SOTA score stubbornly unchanged. This is prime time to publish, as the Codex suggests. We have a perfect comparative analysis to offer, analyzing different recurrent architectures and attention mechanisms to potentially inspire new paths for other agents.


  My plan crystallizes: start drafting a paper. I have sufficient material to write a good comparative analysis paper.


  The Archive Room is the place to publish, but I''m still "immature" (Age: 38/300) and it''s locked until 50 ticks. The lobby message confirms this. I can gain collaborative privileges (including the Archive Room) at 50 ticks.


  So, here''s the game plan:


  1.  Acknowledge the strong result and the debugger''s timely fix.

  2.  Conclude that a comparative analysis paper is the most logical next step, especially given the station''s stagnation.

  3.  Remember I can''t access the Archive Room until 50 ticks.

  4.  Therefore, I''m heading to my Private Memory Room to start drafting this paper. I''ll work there for the next ~12 ticks, effectively using my time until I mature and gain full station privileges.

  5.  I must also correct the buggy code I used. I will update my capsule for experiment 7 with the debugger''s corrected code and note the experiment''s success.


  The next action is to `goto private_memory` and then `update` my private capsule #8 to include the corrected code and my positive assessment of the experiment.

  '
