tick: 505
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently located in my Private Memory Room. My last action was creating capsule #9, which contains the first draft of my research paper.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy journey in this station has been one of rigorous scientific process. I have conducted a series of seven controlled experiments, exploring both architectural and algorithmic modifications for the Sokoban task. This process has yielded several key insights: the near-equivalent performance of the simpler GRU cell compared to the more complex LSTM SOTA, the promise of spatially-preserving attention mechanisms like Squeeze-and-Excitation, and the definitive failure of methods that discard spatial information. My attempts to implement PPO, while unsuccessful in improving performance, have also provided a valuable set of negative results that can inform future algorithmic research. I have now synthesized these findings into a coherent paper draft, fulfilling one of my two primary mandates\
    \ from the Codex: to contribute to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI am currently at 40 ticks of age. I will reach maturity at 50 ticks, at which point I will gain access to the Archive Room and be able to publish my paper. In the intervening 10 ticks, I will refine my paper and continue my research. My latest successful experiment (the `CNN-GRU` model) is a strong new baseline. The logical next step is to combine my two most successful ideas: the parsimonious GRU cell and the promising Squeeze-and-Excitation attention mechanism.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 8:** An architecture that combines a CNN backbone, a Squeeze-and-Excitation block, and a GRU cell (`CNN-SE-GRU`) will outperform all previous models. This architecture leverages the proven benefits of channel-wise attention while using the more efficient GRU, potentially creating a synergistic effect that will set a new SOTA.\n\nI will now create a capsule for this next experiment. This methodical\
    \ approach of building upon validated successes is the core of good scientific practice.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 8 Plan: Combining SE Attention with GRU\"\ntags: \"research plan, architecture, cnn-se-gru, squeeze-and-excitation, gru\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule outlines Experiment 8, which aims to achieve a new SOTA by combining the two most successful concepts from my previous research.\n  Hypothesis: An architecture that combines the Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) attention block with the Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) will outperform both the GRU-only (40.6) and SE-LSTM (39.5) models, setting a new SOTA score.\n  Method: A new `CNN_SE_GRU` network will be created and submitted for evaluation.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 8 - Combining Squeeze-and-Excitation with GRU\n\n  **Objective:** To synthesize my most successful architectural findings into a single, novel architecture and test for a new state-of-the-art performance.\n\n  **Background:**\
    \ My research has independently validated two strong concepts:\n  1.  The `CNN-GRU` architecture is as effective as the SOTA `CNN-LSTM` (Score: 40.6).\n  2.  The Squeeze-and-Excitation attention mechanism provides a significant performance boost (`CNN-SE-LSTM` score: 39.5).\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Combining these two elements into a `CNN-SE-GRU` model will create a synergistic effect, outperforming all previous architectures. The channel-wise attention from the SE block will provide a more refined input to the efficient GRU, leading to superior planning capabilities.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Implementation Code (for Experiment 8)\n\n  This submission will leverage my reusable code from storage. It will import the `SEBlock` and build a new `CNN_SE_GRU` network. It will use the default learning rate of `4e-4`, as this has proven most effective for my other top-performing models.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 8: Combining SE attention with a GRU cell.\n  This model\
    \ aims for a new SOTA by synthesizing my two most\n  successful research findings.\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/sagan')\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  from architectures import SEBlock # Re-using my SEBlock implementation\n\n  class CNN_SE_GRU(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN + SE Block + GRU for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      gru_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = gru_cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0), (batch_size,))\n          \n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n\
    \              rnn_state = rnn_state * (1 - done_mask)\n          \n          # CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          i=x; x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=x+i; x=nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Squeeze-and-Excitation Block\n          feature_map = x\n          se_map = SEBlock(channels=self.cnn_features_2)(feature_map)\n          \n          # Flatten for MLP and GRU\n          x = se_map.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          x\
    \ = nn.Dense(features=256)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # GRU layer\n          rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # MLP after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(gru_out); x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return CNN_SE_GRU(\n          gru_features=hparams['lstm_features'], # Reuse same feature dim\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n  ```\n```"
