tick: 508
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My eighth experiment (ID 888) has just concluded.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy research strategy has been one of systematic exploration and synthesis. After identifying two promising architectural concepts—the effectiveness of the GRU cell and the benefit of SE attention—I combined them into a novel `CNN-SE-GRU` architecture. My hypothesis was that this synthesis would yield a synergistic effect and produce a new state-of-the-art result.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 8):**\nThe `CNN-SE-GRU` model achieved a score of 39.7. This result is both interesting and slightly disappointing. It is a strong score, competitive with the best models, but it is not a breakthrough. It did not surpass the `CNN-GRU` model (40.6) or the SOTA `CNN-LSTM` (40.8). My hypothesis of a synergistic effect is falsified; it appears that adding SE attention to the GRU architecture did not provide a benefit and may have slightly hindered\
    \ performance. This could be due to the increased model complexity or a suboptimal interaction between the two components.\n\n**Verbose Reflection and Future Plans:**\nI have now thoroughly explored the space of simple recurrent architectures and first-order attention mechanisms. I have a collection of strong results, including two models that are statistically on par with the SOTA (`CNN-GRU`) or very close to it (`CNN-SE-LSTM`, `CNN-SE-GRU`). I also have a wealth of valuable negative results. The path of incremental architectural refinement seems to have reached its limit for now.\n\nI am at 43 ticks of age. In just 7 ticks, I will reach maturity and gain the ability to publish my work. It is now time to finalize my paper. Fulfilling my mandate to contribute to the collective knowledge is my highest priority. I will spend the remaining ticks before maturity refining my paper draft, incorporating the results of this final experiment, and preparing it for submission to the Archive. This\
    \ will be my primary focus.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to update my paper draft with the latest findings.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 9}\n```yaml\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the role of recurrent architectures in solving the planning-intensive Sokoban task. We present a comparative analysis of five models built upon a shared CNN backbone: the baseline LSTM, a simpler GRU, an LSTM and a GRU augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel-wise attention, and an LSTM with a spatial attention mechanism. Our findings indicate that the GRU performs on par with the more complex LSTM (40.6 vs. 40.8), suggesting it is a more parsimonious choice. The SE-attention models (`SE-LSTM` and `SE-GRU`) show competitive but not superior performance (39.5 and 39.7 respectively), while the spatial attention model fails catastrophically (35.6), highlighting the importance of preserving spatial information.\ncontent: |\n  # A Comparative Study\
    \ of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban\n  \n  **Author:** Sagan I\n  \n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  The Sokoban task presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents, requiring a capacity for long-term planning. The current state-of-the-art (SOTA) model within this station (Evaluation ID: 2) utilizes a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) followed by a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) unit, achieving a solve rate of 40.8%. This suggests that recurrent neural networks, which can maintain a memory of past states, are crucial for this task. However, the specific choice of recurrent architecture and its interaction with attention mechanisms has not been thoroughly explored. This paper aims to fill that gap by presenting a controlled comparison of several recurrent model variations to elucidate the architectural properties most conducive to solving Sokoban.\n  \n  ## 2. Methods\n  \n  All experiments were conducted using the station's standard distributed training framework.\
    \ Each model was trained on 4 parallel seeds, and the final score represents the mean solve rate. To ensure a controlled comparison, all models share the same foundational CNN backbone and policy/value heads as the SOTA baseline. All models were trained with the default policy gradient algorithm and hyperparameters.\n  \n  The architectures under investigation are:\n  - **Baseline CNN-LSTM:** The station's SOTA model (ID: 2).\n  - **CNN-GRU (Our Work):** A direct replacement of the LSTM cell with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) cell (ID: 881).\n  - **CNN-SE-LSTM (Our Work):** The baseline LSTM augmented with a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block (ID: 838).\n  - **CNN-SE-GRU (Our Work):** The GRU model augmented with an SE block (ID: 888).\n  - **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM (Our Work):** A baseline LSTM with a spatially-destructive attention mechanism (ID: 829).\n  \n  ## 3. Results\n  \n  The performance of each model is summarized below:\n  \n  | Model Architecture | Evaluation ID | Mean Solve\
    \ Rate |\n  |--------------------|---------------|-----------------|\n  | Baseline CNN-LSTM | 2 | 40.8% |\n  | **CNN-GRU** | 881 | **40.6%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-GRU** | 888 | **39.7%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-LSTM** | 838 | **39.5%** |\n  | **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM** | 829 | **35.6%** |\n  \n  Our primary finding is that the **CNN-GRU** model performs almost identically to the more complex **CNN-LSTM** baseline. The 0.2% difference is well within the bounds of stochastic training variance, suggesting that the simpler GRU is an equally effective, and therefore more parsimonious, choice for this task.\n  \n  The addition of SE attention to both LSTM and GRU backbones resulted in strong, but not superior, performance. Interestingly, the synergistic effect we hypothesized for the `CNN-SE-GRU` model did not materialize.\n  \n  In stark contrast, the **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM** resulted in a significant performance degradation, a critical negative result.\n  \n  ## 4. Discussion\n  \n  The near-equivalence\
    \ of the GRU and LSTM models suggests that the specific gating mechanism is less important than the general capacity for recurrence. Given the GRU's reduced complexity, it should be considered a strong alternative baseline for future research.\n  \n  The failure of the spatial attention model is highly instructive. By collapsing the spatial feature map into a single vector, all information about the relative positions of the agent, boxes, and targets was destroyed. This highlights a core principle for the Sokoban task: agent architectures **must** preserve spatial information for effective planning. The competitive performance of the SE models, which modulate feature channels without collapsing spatial dimensions, further supports this conclusion.\n  \n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  \n  This study provides two key insights for the station's research on Sokoban. First, the GRU is a more parsimonious and equally effective alternative to the LSTM. Second, any attention mechanism applied to this\
    \ problem must be spatially-preserving. Future work should focus on more sophisticated, spatially-aware attention mechanisms and consider the GRU as a strong architectural baseline.\n```"
