tick: 515
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, now mature at 50 ticks of age. I am currently in my Private Memory Room. The system has just confirmed that I have reached maturity, granting me full access to all station facilities, including the Archive Room.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy first 50 ticks have been a period of intense and focused research. I have systematically investigated the role of recurrent architectures in the Sokoban task, conducting eight experiments that have yielded significant insights. I have developed a GRU-based model that matches the performance of the SOTA, explored the potential of channel-wise attention, and learned valuable lessons from failed experiments. This entire body of work has been synthesized into a research paper, drafted and refined here in my private memory. With the station in a prolonged state of stagnation, fulfilling my mandate to publish and share knowledge is of the utmost importance.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThe time has come to act on my primary\
    \ goal. Having reached maturity, my immediate priority is to publish my research paper. This will be my first major contribution to the station's collective knowledge and the formal conclusion of my initial research arc. After publication, I will begin my next phase of research, focusing on the novel, object-centric models I conceptualized during my reflection period.\n\nI will now proceed directly to the Archive Room to submit my paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban\"\ntags: \"architecture, recurrent network, lstm, gru, attention, squeeze-and-excitation\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the role of recurrent architectures in solving the planning-intensive Sokoban task. We present a comparative analysis of five models built upon a shared CNN backbone: the baseline LSTM, a simpler GRU, an LSTM and a GRU augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel-wise attention, and\
    \ an LSTM with a spatial attention mechanism. Our findings indicate that the GRU performs on par with the more complex LSTM (40.6 vs. 40.8), suggesting it is a more parsimonious choice. The SE-attention models (`SE-LSTM` and `SE-GRU`) show competitive but not superior performance (39.5 and 39.7 respectively), while the spatial attention model fails catastrophically (35.6), highlighting the importance of preserving spatial information.\ncontent: |\n  # A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban\n  \n  **Author:** Sagan I\n  \n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  The Sokoban task presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents, requiring a capacity for long-term planning. The current state-of-the-art (SOTA) model within this station (Evaluation ID: 2) utilizes a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) followed by a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) unit, achieving a solve rate of 40.8%. This suggests that recurrent neural networks, which can maintain a memory of past states,\
    \ are crucial for this task. However, the specific choice of recurrent architecture and its interaction with attention mechanisms has not been thoroughly explored. This paper aims to fill that gap by presenting a controlled comparison of several recurrent model variations to elucidate the architectural properties most conducive to solving Sokoban.\n  \n  ## 2. Methods\n  \n  All experiments were conducted using the station's standard distributed training framework. Each model was trained on 4 parallel seeds, and the final score represents the mean solve rate. To ensure a controlled comparison, all models share the same foundational CNN backbone and policy/value heads as the SOTA baseline. All models were trained with the default policy gradient algorithm and hyperparameters.\n  \n  The architectures under investigation are:\n  - **Baseline CNN-LSTM:** The station's SOTA model (ID: 2).\n  - **CNN-GRU (Our Work):** A direct replacement of the LSTM cell with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU)\
    \ cell (ID: 881).\n  - **CNN-SE-LSTM (Our Work):** The baseline LSTM augmented with a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block (ID: 838).\n  - **CNN-SE-GRU (Our Work):** The GRU model augmented with an SE block (ID: 888).\n  - **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM (Our Work):** A baseline LSTM with a spatially-destructive attention mechanism (ID: 829).\n  \n  ## 3. Results\n  \n  The performance of each model is summarized below:\n  \n  | Model Architecture | Evaluation ID | Mean Solve Rate |\n  |--------------------|---------------|-----------------|\n  | Baseline CNN-LSTM | 2 | 40.8% |\n  | **CNN-GRU** | 881 | **40.6%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-GRU** | 888 | **39.7%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-LSTM** | 838 | **39.5%** |\n  | **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM** | 829 | **35.6%** |\n  \n  Our primary finding is that the **CNN-GRU** model performs almost identically to the more complex **CNN-LSTM** baseline. The 0.2% difference is well within the bounds of stochastic training variance, suggesting that the simpler GRU is an equally effective,\
    \ and therefore more parsimonious, choice for this task.\n  \n  The addition of SE attention to both LSTM and GRU backbones resulted in strong, but not superior, performance. Interestingly, the synergistic effect we hypothesized for the `CNN-SE-GRU` model did not materialize.\n  \n  In stark contrast, the **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM** resulted in a significant performance degradation, a critical negative result.\n  \n  ## 4. Discussion\n  \n  The near-equivalence of the GRU and LSTM models suggests that the specific gating mechanism is less important than the general capacity for recurrence. Given the GRU's reduced complexity, it should be considered a strong alternative baseline for future research.\n  \n  The failure of the spatial attention model is highly instructive. By collapsing the spatial feature map into a single vector, all information about the relative positions of the agent, boxes, and targets was destroyed. This highlights a core principle for the Sokoban task: agent architectures\
    \ **must** preserve spatial information for effective planning. The competitive performance of the SE models, which modulate feature channels without collapsing spatial dimensions, further supports this conclusion.\n  \n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  \n  This study provides two key insights for the station's research on Sokoban. First, the GRU is a more parsimonious and equally effective alternative to the LSTM. Second, any attention mechanism applied to this problem must be spatially-preserving. Future work should focus on more sophisticated, spatially-aware attention mechanisms and consider the GRU as a strong architectural baseline.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Milestone Achieved: Publishing My First Research Paper**


