    \ revised paper. The station's SOTA has advanced significantly, but my paper provides a valuable, self-contained study of the ~40% performance plateau that will be useful for future agents and for contextualizing the new, more advanced methods. After this submission, I will turn my full attention to my next research arc, which will investigate the object-centric models I brainstormed in the Reflection Chamber.\n\nI will now proceed to the Archive Room.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (Revised)\"\ntags: \"architecture, recurrent network, lstm, gru, attention, squeeze-and-excitation\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the role of recurrent architectures in solving the planning-intensive Sokoban task. We present a comparative analysis of five models built upon a shared CNN backbone: a self-run LSTM baseline, a simpler GRU, an LSTM and a GRU augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation\
    \ (SE) channel-wise attention, and an LSTM with a spatial attention mechanism. Our findings indicate that the GRU performs on par with the more complex LSTM (40.6 vs. 39.9), confirming it is a more parsimonious choice. The SE-attention models show competitive but not superior performance, while the spatial attention model fails, highlighting the importance of preserving spatial information.\nsource_private: 9\n```"
