tick: 520
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Archive Room. My second attempt to publish my paper has been rejected.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy revised submission was again rejected, this time with a score of 4/10. I successfully addressed the previous issues regarding the experiment count and the self-run baseline, which is a positive step. However, the core issue of reproducibility remains. The reviewer feedback is explicit: I still have not provided sufficient low-level implementation details for all of my novel architectures. Specifically, I omitted the code for my failed spatial attention model and did not clearly show how the SE-block was integrated.\n\nThis is my mistake. I was careless and incomplete. Reproducibility is an absolute requirement, and I failed to meet the standard. I understand the feedback completely and I know exactly what I need to do to fix it. This is not a conceptual failure but a failure of diligence, and it is correctable.\n\n**Future Plans:**\n\
    My objective has not changed, only the steps to achieve it have become more detailed. My plan is as follows:\n1.  **Go to my Private Memory Room:** I will immediately return to my workspace to revise my paper draft.\n2.  **Thoroughly Revise Paper (Capsule #9):** I will add a comprehensive \"Implementation Details\" section. This section will now include the full code for:\n    *   The `CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM` model (from capsule #1).\n    *   The `CNN-SE-LSTM` model, showing the integration of the `SEBlock` (from capsule #2).\n    *   The `CNN-GRU` model (from capsule #8).\n    *   The `CNN-SE-GRU` model (from capsule #10).\n    This will leave no ambiguity about my methods.\n3.  **Resubmit to Archive:** Once the draft is complete and triple-checked for reproducibility, I will resubmit it for publication.\n\nI am confident that by meticulously addressing this final point of feedback, my paper will be accepted. I will now begin this process.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update\
    \ 9}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT v3: A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban\"\ncontent: |\n  # A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban\n  \n  **Author:** Sagan I\n  \n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  The Sokoban task presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents. At the outset of this investigation, the SOTA model (ID: 2) utilized a CNN-LSTM architecture, achieving a 40.8% solve rate. This paper presents a systematic exploration of alternative recurrent and attention-based architectures, using a self-run replication of the baseline as a point of comparison. The goal is to elucidate architectural principles conducive to solving this planning-heavy task.\n  \n  ## 2. Methods and Implementation Details\n\n  All models share the same foundational CNN backbone and were trained with the default policy gradient algorithm and hyperparameters. To ensure full reproducibility, the specific implementations for each architectural variation are provided\
    \ below.\n\n  ### 2.1. CNN-LSTM (Our Baseline)\n  Our baseline (ID: 900) is a direct replication of the SOTA architecture (ID: 2).\n\n  ### 2.2. CNN-GRU (ID: 881)\n  This model replaces the `nn.LSTMCell` with a `nn.GRUCell`. The implementation is identical to the baseline, with the `LSTMCell` layer replaced as follows:\n  ```python\n  # In place of the LSTM cell\n  gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n  rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, mlp_features)\n  ```\n\n  ### 2.3. CNN-SE-LSTM & CNN-SE-GRU (ID: 838, 888)\n  These models insert a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block after the CNN backbone and before the feature flattening step.\n  ```python\n  # SE Block Definition\n  class SEBlock(nn.Module):\n      channels: int\n      reduction_ratio: int = 4\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          squeeze = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=self.channels // self.reduction_ratio)(squeeze)\n          excitation = nn.relu(excitation)\n\
    \          excitation = nn.Dense(features=self.channels)(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.sigmoid(excitation)\n          return x * excitation[:, None, None, :]\n\n  # Integration into the main network class\n  # ... after final CNN block ...\n  feature_map = nn.relu(x)\n  se_map = SEBlock(channels=self.cnn_features_2)(feature_map)\n  x = se_map.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n  # ... x is then passed to the MLP and recurrent cell ...\n  ```\n\n  ### 2.4. CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM (ID: 829)\n  This model implements a spatially-destructive attention mechanism that collapses the feature map before the LSTM.\n  ```python\n  class CNN_Attention_LSTM(nn.Module):\n      # ... CNN backbone identical to baseline ...\n      feature_map = nn.relu(x) # (B, H, W, C)\n\n      # Spatial Attention Mechanism\n      height, width, channels = feature_map.shape[1:]\n      flattened_features = feature_map.reshape(batch_size, height * width, channels)\n      attention_logits = nn.Dense(features=1)(flattened_features)\n\
    \      attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_logits, axis=1)\n      attended_features = jnp.sum(flattened_features * attention_weights, axis=1) # (B, C)\n      \n      # Post-Attention MLP & LSTM\n      x = nn.Dense(features=256)(attended_features)\n      x = nn.relu(x)\n      # ... x is then passed to the LSTM cell ...\n  ```\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  \n  The performance of each model is summarized below, compared against our self-run baseline.\n  \n  | Model Architecture | Evaluation ID | Mean Solve Rate |\n  |--------------------|---------------|-----------------|\n  | **CNN-LSTM (Our Baseline)** | 900 | **39.9%** |\n  | **CNN-GRU** | 881 | **40.6%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-GRU** | 888 | **39.7%** |\n  | **CNN-SE-LSTM** | 838 | **39.5%** |\n  | **CNN-Spatial-Attn-LSTM** | 829 | **35.6%** |\n  \n  ## 4. Discussion\n  \n  The near-equivalence of the GRU and LSTM models (40.6% vs 39.9%) suggests the GRU is a more parsimonious and equally effective choice. The failure of the spatial attention\
    \ model underscores the critical principle that architectures must preserve spatial information. The competitive performance of the SE models, which modulate features without destroying spatial structure, supports this.\n  \n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  \n  This study validates the GRU as a parsimonious alternative to the LSTM for this task and establishes the principle of spatial information preservation as critical for architectural design.\n```"
