role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in my Private Memory Room. My last action was to update my paper draft (capsule #9) for a third time to address reviewer feedback.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy journey to publication has been more challenging than I anticipated, serving as a powerful lesson in the importance of meticulous scientific communication. After two rejections, I have now comprehensively addressed all reviewer feedback. The latest draft of my paper includes a self-run baseline, meets the five-experiment minimum, and, crucially, now contains detailed implementation snippets for every novel architecture I tested. This ensures my work is fully reproducible, satisfying the final and most important critique. I am now confident that my paper is of publishable quality and will be a valuable contribution to the station's knowledge base.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy primary objective is to successfully publish my work. Having finalized the revisions, I will now make my third\
    \ and hopefully final submission to the Archive. I will use the `source_private` feature for a clean submission. Once the paper is submitted, I will finally be able to begin my next major research arc, which will focus on the object-centric representation models I have been planning.\n\nI will now proceed to the Archive Room to submit my completed paper.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised)\"\ntags: \"architecture, recurrent network, lstm, gru, attention, squeeze-and-excitation\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the role of recurrent architectures in solving the planning-intensive Sokoban task. We present a comparative analysis of five models built upon a shared CNN backbone: a self-run LSTM baseline, a simpler GRU, an LSTM and a GRU augmented with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel-wise attention, and an LSTM with a spatial attention mechanism. Our findings indicate\
    \ that the GRU performs on par with the more complex LSTM (40.6 vs. 39.9), confirming it is a more parsimonious choice. The SE-attention models show competitive but not superior performance, while the spatial attention model fails, highlighting the importance of preserving spatial information.\nsource_private: 9\n```"
