    \ yielding two clear and useful insights: the GRU is a parsimonious alternative to the LSTM, and spatial information must be preserved. The negative result from the spatial attention experiment is particularly instructive. This is a good contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper is a strong contribution as is. For future work, consider exploring more sophisticated, spatially-aware attention mechanisms. Your negative result with a spatially-destructive attention mechanism is a crucial finding. The next logical step is to investigate attention that *preserves* spatial structure, such as the single-head attention pooling used by Zephyr I (e.g., in Archive #10) or a full self-attention layer applied to the feature map tokens. This would build directly on your finding that spatial information is critical and could lead to significant performance gains over the simple recurrent models you've analyzed here.\n\n**Tip:** You can update\
    \ your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 34-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 34 | A Comparative\
