tick: 523
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Archive Room. My last action was to preview all available research papers to catch up on the station''s progress.


    **Recap and Reflection:**

    My literature review has been illuminating and humbling. While I was focused on my initial research arc, which plateaued around a 40% solve rate, the other agents in the station have made monumental progress, with SOTA scores now exceeding 94%. The key themes of this progress are clear:

    1.  **ConvLSTM:** The simple recurrent units I studied (LSTM/GRU) have been superseded by `ConvLSTM`, which maintains spatial structure within the recurrent state. This is a powerful idea that directly addresses the principle of spatial preservation I identified.

    2.  **Normalization:** Layer Normalization (LN), particularly on the ConvLSTM gates and inputs, appears to be a critical component for stabilizing training and enabling high performance. Zephyr II''s "Residual Input-Normalization" (RIN) seems especially potent.

    3.  **Attention:** The simple attention mechanisms I explored are primitive compared to the `Attention⊕GAP` pooling method used by Zephyr II, which seems to be a key part of their high-performing PG agent.

    4.  **PPO:** Proximal Policy Optimization has been successfully implemented and is a key driver of many SOTA results, but its performance is highly sensitive to the architecture, particularly the complexity of the policy/value heads.

    5.  **Context is King:** A recurring theme across many papers is context-dependency. An architectural component that works in one setting can fail or even be detrimental in another. This reinforces the need for rigorous, controlled experimentation.

    6.  **My own research:** I was particularly interested to see a paper by Insight I (Archive #18 and #23) that replicated my Squeeze-and-Excitation idea on SOTA backbones. They found it led to a *performance degradation*, a "negative synergy." This is a fascinating and valuable finding that adds context to my own work.


    **Future Plans:**

    My planned research arc on object-centric models (Slot Attention, GNNs) remains a valid, novel direction. However, to simply ignore the incredible progress made by others would be unscientific. The Codex encourages independent thought but also the thoughtful assimilation of external knowledge.


    Therefore, I will adjust my plan. Before diving into a completely novel paradigm like Slot Attention, I will first attempt to build upon the successful foundations laid by the other agents. My first step will be to replicate the core components of the current SOTA. My next experiment will be to implement a `ConvLSTM` architecture with Layer Normalization. This will serve as my new, much stronger, personal baseline. It will allow me to understand the mechanics of these advanced models firsthand before I try to innovate on top of them.


    First, I need to read the details of these papers, not just the abstracts. I will start with Zephyr II''s paper on the 94.3% PG agent, as it seems to be a foundational work.


    /execute_action{read 24}'
