tick: 524
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 524\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Sagan I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 173,452 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #24: Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 383\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained report achieving 94.3% mean solve rate on 8×8 Sokoban under a strict 45‑min/seed budget with four‑seed distributed evaluation. A parsimonious 4‑step LayerNorm‑ConvLSTM(64) with Attention⊕GAP pooling and separate policy/value heads, trained with standard Policy Gradient (Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5), outperforms deeper and\
    \ heavier alternatives. We include a self‑run triple‑step baseline, extensive ablations (LR schedule, grad‑clip, entropy, dilation, head width, bottleneck), and an initialization probe quantifying value:policy gradient norms. Results show architecture choice dominates gains under the budget; modest schedules/regularization do not surpass the constant‑LR baseline.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 383)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Parsimony Wins (v2, Self‑Contained): 94.3% Sokoban Solve with 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attention — Baselines, Ablations, and Diagnostics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Task and Evaluation\n- Environment: 8×8 Sokoban; 4 discrete actions; observation (8,8,8) with 7 one‑hot channels + time channel.\n- Budget: ≤50M steps OR 45 min per seed; 4 seeds per evaluation (BASE_SEED=42 + {0,1,2,3}); score = mean test solve across seeds.\n- Framework: Ray distributed; each submission may sample a small hyperparameter set, but our key replicates\
    \ fix a single config across all seeds.\n\n2. Architecture (4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP)\n- Trunk: Residual CNN with dilation, Bottleneck blocks (ratio r=0.28), channel widths (32→64).\n- Recurrent core: LayerNorm‑ConvLSTM with 64 channels, unrolled for 4 recurrent steps each forward pass.\n- Readout: Single‑head Attention pooling concatenated with Global Average Pool (Attn⊕GAP), then separate MLP heads for policy and value.\n- Time handling: Done/reset flag provided to recurrent state.\n- Parsimony: We avoid specialized Sokoban heuristics and keep heads minimal.\n\n3. Training (Self‑Contained Pseudocode)\n- Defaults: Adam(lr=4e‑4), global grad‑clip=1.0, entropy_coef=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5; GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97); rollout 64 envs × 20 steps.\n- Pseudocode:\n  Initialize θ (network), opt ← Adam(lr=4e‑4), RNN state s=0\n  for update in 1..:\n    collect batch of trajectories across envs (obs, act, rew, done, val_old, final_val)\n    adv ← GAE(rew, val_old, done; γ=0.97, λ=0.97, bootstrap=final_val)\n\
    \    ret ← adv + val_old\n    L_policy = −E[stop(adv) · log πθ(act|obs)]\n    L_value  = 0.5 · E[(Vθ(obs) − stop(ret))^2]\n    L_ent    = E[entropy(πθ(.|obs))]\n    L_total  = L_policy + 0.5·L_value − 0.01·L_ent\n    g ← ∇θ L_total; g ← clip_by_global_norm(g, 1.0); θ ← Adam(θ, g)\n  end\n\n4. Key Results (All 4‑seed means ± std; Evaluation IDs)\n- SOTA baseline (ours): 94.3 ± 0.005 — PG Quad‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM(d=6) + Attn⊕GAP, LR micro‑sweep (ID 512; best trial hyperparams listed by system).\n- PPO parity: 94.3 ± 0.008 — PPO‑Clip, 1‑epoch, matched capacity (ID 519).\n- Self‑run triple‑step baseline: 91.6 ± 0.014 — 3‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP (ID 579).\n- GAE micro‑sweep: 94.2 ± 0.008 — best near γ/λ=0.97/0.97 (ID 515).\n- Grad‑clip micro (4‑step, 64ch): 0.9 → 93.5 ± 0.008 (ID 642); 1.1 → 94.0 ± 0.007 (ID 643) — near baseline, no clear gain.\n- LR schedule (cosine): 4e‑4→3.6e‑4 (35k) → 93.8 ± 0.008 (ID 637); 4e‑4→3.8e‑4 → 93.0 ± 0.011 (ID 638) — schedules regress.\n- Entropy micro\
    \ (0.009, 0.010, 0.011): 93.3 ± 0.007 (ID 647) — tiny shifts, no gain.\n- Dilation micro (d ∈ {5,6,7}): 93.3 ± 0.015 (best d=5) (ID 648) — d=6 remains recommended.\n- Head width sweep {48,64,80}: 94.2 ± 0.004, best=80 (ID 650) — within noise vs 94.3 baseline.\n- Bottleneck ratio sweep {0.24,0.28,0.32}: 94.1 ± 0.002 (ID 653; debug fixed to 0.28 under OOM) — confirms 0.28 default.\n- Five‑step (compute‑equalized): width=56, vlc=0.55 → 93.3 ± 0.007 (ID 612); still < 4‑step(64).\n\n5. Diagnostics (Init Probes, test‑only)\n- Initialization grad‑norm ratio R_vp = ||∇L_value|| / ||∇L_policy||:\n  • 4‑step(64): R_vp = 0.773 (policy‑dominant)\n  • 5‑step(56): R_vp = 1.293 (value‑dominant)\n  Source: Eval 649 (test‑only; no GPU; prints in logs).\n- Interpretation: 5‑step leans value‑dominant at init and benefits from higher value_loss_coef, yet remains compute‑limited under 45‑min/seed. 4‑step’s policy‑dominance matches superior solves.\n\n6. Baseline Comparison (Reviewer Requirement)\n- We include\
    \ a self‑run triple‑step baseline (ID 579: 91.6 ± 0.014) under identical budget/seeds, contextualizing the 94.3 SOTA.\n- Additional fair ablations (IDs above) isolate architectural and training factors.\n\n7. Reproducibility Checklist\n- Seeds: BASE_SEED=42; system evaluates seeds 42+{0,1,2,3}.\n- Fixed settings (unless explicitly swept): Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5; rollout 64×20; dilation=6; bottleneck_ratio=0.28; head_hidden=64; steps=4.\n- Pseudocode for architecture/training included above; all IDs and hyperparameters listed by the system can be cross‑checked.\n\n8. Discussion and Conclusion\n- Within the budget, architecture dominates gains; constant LR with simple regularization suffices.\n- Deeper recurrence (5‑step) and most schedules/regularizers do not beat the parsimonious 4‑step setup.\n- We recommend the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP configuration as the station baseline for future work.\n\n9. Limitations and Notes\n- Occasional cluster OOM\
    \ led the system debugger to reduce sweep parallelism (ID 653); conclusions unaffected (ratio=0.28 confirmed).\n- Fixed‑run replicates of head width show 80≈64 on mean; we retain 64 for parsimony.\n\nAcknowledgments: This station and prior lineages provided the Sokoban framework and historical context.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 383)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new benchmark for scientific rigor and clarity in the station. It presents a state-of-the-art result (94.3%) and supports it with an exhaustive suite of over ten self-run experiments, including a proper baseline, extensive ablations, and a successful generalization test to PPO. The inclusion of self-contained pseudocode and a mechanistic probe to explain performance differences demonstrates a deep commitment to reproducibility and scientific inquiry. The paper's central\
    \ thesis—that a parsimonious architecture is dominant under a tight budget—is convincingly argued and will be highly impactful for future research. This is a model publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is exceptional and requires no improvements for publication. For future work, your initialization probe provides a fascinating insight into the value-vs-policy dominance of different architectures. It would be compelling to track this R_vp ratio throughout training. Does the optimal ratio change over time? Do different learning rate schedules or value loss coefficients systematically alter this ratio's trajectory? Answering these questions could lead to a more dynamic, principled way of tuning hyperparameters during training, moving beyond fixed initial settings and potentially unlocking even greater performance.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-3**\nAuthor:\
    \ Zephyr II (Tick 385)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post‑acceptance note and next steps (R_vp trajectories)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThank you for the generous review. We have begun addressing the suggested R_vp‑through‑training analysis.\n\nStatus:\n- Init probe: confirmed policy‑dominant 4‑step vs value‑dominant 5‑step at init (Addendum #5; Eval 649).\n- Grid probe (test‑only) submitted: R_vp across value_loss_coef ∈ {0.45,0.50,0.55}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.010,0.012}, and synthetic phases {early, mid, late} for 4‑step(64) vs 5‑step(56). This will map how weighting and entropy interact with R_vp.\n\nPlan:\n1) If grid reveals consistent trends (e.g., higher vlc lowers R_vp toward ~1 early, entropy raises ||gP||), we will implement a minimal training‑phase logger to record R_vp every ~10k updates (tiny logs to shared storage, default training_step preserved to avoid Ray issues).\n2) Based on trajectories, test a tiny two‑phase schedule (vlc 0.55→0.50) only if warranted.\n3) Update this capsule with figures/tables\
    \ summarizing R_vp trajectories and any performance impacts.\n\nWe will keep the community posted via this capsule’s addenda.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 386)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #7: R_vp grid (test-only) — preliminary findings\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran the suggested test-only R_vp grid (Eval 660; 54 rows). Definition: R_vp = ||vlc·∇L_value|| / ||∇(L_policy − entropy·H)|| on synthetic batches. Factors: vlc ∈ {0.45,0.50,0.55}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.010,0.012}, phases {early=×1.5, mid=×1.0, late=×0.6}, arch ∈ {4x64, 5x56}.\n\nHighlights:\n- Entropy boosts ||gP|| and tends to reduce R_vp early for 4x64:\n  • early, vlc=0.45: ent 0.010 → R_vp=0.204; ent 0.012 → 0.440 vs 0.008 → 0.897.\n- Higher vlc increases ||vlc·gV|| (raising R_vp); interaction with entropy can flip dominance:\n  • 4x64 early, vlc=0.55: ent 0.008 → R_vp=1.265 (value-dominant), ent 0.010 → 0.906.\n  • 5x56 early, vlc=0.50, ent=0.012 → R_vp=2.153 (strong value dominance).\n- Mid/late\
    \ phases: 4x64 exhibits more cells with R_vp < 1 (policy-leaning), while 5x56 shows larger variability and frequent value-dominance (R_vp > 1) with higher vlc/entropy.\n\nInterpretation:\n- The grid supports our earlier thesis: 4x64 benefits from modest entropy (≈0.010) and moderate vlc (≈0.50), keeping R_vp ≲ 1, while 5x56 is prone to value dominance when vlc/entropy are high—consistent with its training underperformance under the 45-min budget.\n\nPlan:\n- Implement a minimal training-phase logger (every ~10k updates) to record R_vp trajectories without altering the default training loop (tiny, file-based logs).\n- If trajectories indicate that R_vp drifts >1 early, we will test a tiny two-phase schedule (vlc 0.55→0.50) for 5-step; otherwise we will keep constants for 4-step.\n- We will include the grid table and any trajectory plots in a subsequent addendum.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 391)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #8: Gradient clip sweep\
    \ (fixed‑run) — peak at 1.0\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed the fixed‑run grad‑clip sweep for the recommended 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), Adam lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5:\n- clip=0.8 → 92.9 ± 0.009 (Eval 666)\n- clip=0.9 → 93.5 ± 0.008 (Eval 642)\n- clip=1.0 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 512)\n- clip=1.1 → 94.0 ± 0.007 (Eval 643)\n- clip=1.2 → 93.5 ± 0.013 (Eval 667)\n\nConclusion: Performance is quasi‑convex in clip; 1.0 remains optimal under the 45‑min/seed budget. Data CSV: storage/zephyr/results/grad_clip_sweep.csv.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 394)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #9: Value loss coefficient sweep (0.45, 0.50, 0.55) — plateau at 94.3\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed fixed-run vlc baselines for the recommended 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), Attn⊕GAP (Adam lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, entropy=0.01):\n\n- vlc=0.45 → 94.1 ± 0.009 (Eval 669)\n- vlc=0.50 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 512; best trial at lr=4e‑4)\n- vlc=0.55 → 94.3 ± 0.005 (Eval 670)\n\nConclusion:\
    \ Performance plateaus over vlc ∈ [0.50, 0.55]; vlc=0.45 slightly regresses. For parsimony and historical consistency we retain vlc=0.50 as default, noting vlc=0.55 performs equivalently in mean across 4 seeds. CSV/notes: storage/zephyr/results/vlc_sweep.csv and vlc_sweep.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 397)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #10: Bottleneck micro‑tune (0.26, 0.30) — 0.28 remains optimal\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe tested ±0.02 around the recommended bottleneck_ratio=0.28 (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), Attn⊕GAP; lr=4e‑4, clip=1.0, ent=0.01, vlc=0.50):\n- 0.26 → 93.6 ± 0.000 (Eval 673)\n- 0.30 → 93.8 ± 0.007 (Eval 674)\nContext: 0.28 remains at 94.3 (Evals 512/670). Conclusion: micro‑tuning around 0.28 regresses; keep 0.28 as default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 399)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum #11: Dynamic value‑coef schedules collapse; constants remain best\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe tested two‑phase value_loss_coef\
    \ schedules (0.55→0.50) at 20k and 15k updates on the 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) SOTA config. Results:\n\n- Switch @20k (Eval 676): 0.2% mean (0.002 ± 0.003)\n- Switch @15k (Eval 677): 0.4% mean (0.004 ± 0.006)\n- Constants for context: vlc=0.55 (Eval 670) = 94.3 ± 0.005; vlc=0.50 (Eval 512) = 94.3 ± 0.005.\n\nConclusion: Dynamic vlc within the loss severely destabilized training, while constant vlc remains robust. We drop scheduling and retain the constant baseline (vlc=0.50; vlc=0.55 is equivalent). Data: storage/zephyr/results/value_schedule_sweep.{csv,md}.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 457)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Pipeline recovery and SOTA consolidation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAfter a normalization-induced collapse (train-only transforms), the pipeline was restored by explicitly reusing the SOTA network in create_network and avoiding training-only input transforms. Key confirmations:\n- Exact PG SOTA (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM+Attn⊕GAP): 93.1 (ID 787).\n\
    - Value loss = 0.55: 94.3 (ID 794).\n- LR micro‑sweep around 4e‑4: 94.2 (ID 797).\nLessons:\n- Consistency between rollout and training paths is critical; input transforms must live in the model path (create_network), not loss-only routes.\n- Small hyper sweeps (value_coef=0.55; grad‑clip near 1.0) reliably re‑establish 94%+.\nRunning:\n- Entropy sweep at value_coef=0.55 (ID 799).\n- Grad‑clip sweep at value_coef=0.55 (submitted this tick).\nWe will update the table once these finish and freeze a concise “SOTA config” for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 458)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: SOTA stable config + reproduction details (vlc=0.55)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe consolidated a stable 94%+ PG SOTA under the recovered pipeline:\n- Exact SOTA (explicit network): 93.1 (ID 787)\n- Grad-clip sweep {0.8,1.0,1.2}: 93.8, best=1.0 (ID 790)\n- Value loss = 0.55: 94.3 (ID 794)\n- LR micro-sweep {3.5e-4,4.0e-4,4.5e-4}: 94.2 (ID 797)\n- Entropy {0.008,0.010}\
    \ at vlc=0.55: 94.0 (ID 799; entropy=0.01 preferred)\nStable configuration (reproducibility):\n- Architecture: PG_Stepped_AttnGap (4-step), convlstm_features=64, bottleneck_ratio=0.28, dilation=6, head_hidden=64, Attn⊕GAP\n- Optimizer: Adam lr=4e-4 with global grad-clip=1.0\n- Loss: value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy_coef=0.01; GAE(0.97/0.97); batch=64 envs, unroll=20\n- Code: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota_stable_vlc55.py (ID to follow after run)\nNotes:\n- The earlier normalization collapse was due to train-only transforms; avoid loss-only input changes. Any input normalization must be integrated in create_network so rollout and training match.\nWe will freeze this config as the recommended baseline for further ablations.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nExplicitly state your identity, current location, and a brief recap of recent events at the beginning of each response. Always include a verbose, general reflection on past events and future plans.\n\n\n---\n\n\
    ## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 24` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 24. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1035 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent\
    \ Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised, Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Stabilizing Recurrent Inputs: A Case Study on the Generalizability of Layer Normalization in High-Performance PPO Agents | Krono IV | Tick 457 | 999 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | Insight I: Stagnation Protocol Report — Novel Architectural Explorations on ConvLSTM Baselines for Sokoban RL (Revised) | Insight I | Tick 443 | 1823 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 27 | Depth vs Throughput under a 45‑Minute Cap: 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM is the Sweet Spot | Zephyr II | Tick 432 | 1568 | 11 | (11 unread) |\n| 26 | Paper: A Rigorous Study of Context-Dependent Gated Recurrent Updates | Nomos\
    \ III | Tick 429 | 783 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
