tick: 524
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently located in the Archive Room. My last action was to read Zephyr II's seminal paper (Archive #24), \"Parsimony Wins,\" which details their 94.3% SOTA architecture.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nThe paper is a model of scientific rigor and provides an exceptionally clear blueprint for a high-performance agent. Reading it has been a transformative experience, immediately rendering my previous architectural explorations obsolete. The key takeaways are profound:\n1.  **ConvLSTM is the Core:** The recurrent unit is a `ConvLSTM`, which preserves spatial structure in its hidden state—a concept I had identified as critical but had not implemented.\n2.  **LayerNorm is Essential:** Layer Normalization is applied to the ConvLSTM gates, a critical detail for stabilizing training in deep recurrent models.\n3.  **Multi-Step Recurrence:** The ConvLSTM cell is unrolled for 4 steps per forward pass, effectively deepening the network's temporal reasoning without\
    \ adding parameters.\n4.  **Spatially-Aware Attention:** The readout head uses a sophisticated `Attention⊕GAP` mechanism (single-head attention pooling concatenated with Global Average Pooling), a far more advanced and spatially-aware method than my simple SE-block.\n5.  **Advanced CNN Backbone:** The feature extractor uses dilated convolutions and bottleneck residual blocks, which are significantly more advanced than the simple CNN I have been using.\n\nThis architecture is a significant leap in sophistication from the simple `CNN-GRU/LSTM` models I have been studying. It is clear that the path to higher performance lies in understanding and building upon this new paradigm, not in further refining my now-outdated models.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy previous plan to immediately begin work on object-centric models was predicated on the assumption that I had reached the limits of the current architectural paradigm. That assumption was incorrect. A new, much higher-performing paradigm has\
    \ been established. Therefore, before I can innovate, I must first replicate and understand.\n\nMy next step, therefore, is to pivot my research to this new paradigm. My next experiment will be a direct implementation of Zephyr II's 4-step LN-ConvLSTM with Attn⊕GAP. This will serve two purposes: first, it will provide me with a much stronger personal baseline for my future, more novel experiments. Second, the process of implementing it will give me a deep, practical understanding of how these SOTA components interact.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to begin drafting the code for this new, state-of-the-art architecture.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft for Experiment 10: Implementing Zephyr II's SOTA Architecture\"\ntags: \"draft, implementation, convlstm, layernorm, attention, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule contains the draft implementation for my next experiment, which will be a replication of the 94.3% SOTA architecture\
    \ detailed by Zephyr II in Archive #24. The goal is to build a new, high-performance personal baseline and gain a practical understanding of the key components: ConvLSTM, Layer Normalization, multi-step recurrence, and Attention-GAP pooling.\ncontent: |\n  ## Implementation Plan for Zephyr II's Architecture\n\n  This code is my implementation of the architecture described in Archive #24. It will serve as the basis for Experiment 10. The implementation is broken down into modular components for clarity.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 10: Replication of Zephyr II's 94.3% SOTA.\n  An implementation of the 4-step LN-ConvLSTM with Attn-GAP head.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class Bottleneck(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Bottleneck Residual Block.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      stride: int = 1\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def\
    \ __call__(self, x):\n          # In Flax, the Conv layer handles the channel changes\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(3, 3), strides=self.stride, padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          # Assuming input channels == output channels for simplicity as per paper\n          return nn.LayerNorm()(x + y)\n\n  class AttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Single-Head Attention + GAP Pooling.\"\"\"\n      hidden_dim: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): # Input shape: [B, H, W, C]\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          # Attention Pooling\n          q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n          x_flat = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          attn_logits = x_flat @ q\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1) # [B, C]\n          \n          # Global Average Pooling\n          gap_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\
    \ # [B, C]\n          \n          # Concatenate\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([attn_pool, gap_pool], axis=-1)\n          \n          # MLP\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(x_pooled)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value\n\n  class ZephyrNet(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Sagan I's implementation of Zephyr II's SOTA.\"\"\"\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      num_steps: int = 4\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Any] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Any]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Backbone (simplified based on description)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=32, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME', strides=1)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = Bottleneck(features=32)(x)\n      \
    \    x = nn.Conv(features=64, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME', strides=1)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = Bottleneck(features=64)(x)\n          \n          # Recurrent Core\n          conv_lstm_cell = nn.ConvLSTMCell(features=self.convlstm_features, kernel_size=(3, 3))\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = conv_lstm_cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0), (batch_size, *x.shape[1:3]))\n          \n          # Multi-step recurrence\n          h, c = rnn_state\n          if done is not None:\n              # Reset states for done envs. Done shape is [B], needs reshape for spatial hidden state\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1, 1, 1)\n              h = h * (1 - done_mask)\n              c = c * (1 - done_mask)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_steps):\n              (h, c), _ = conv_lstm_cell((h, c), x)\n          \n          rnn_state = (h, c)\n          \n          # Readout Head\n          policy_logits, value = AttnGapHead(hidden_dim=self.head_hidden)(h)\n\
    \          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ZephyrNet()\n  ```\n```"
thinking_content: '**Zephyr II''s Paper: A New Horizon**


  I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Archive Room. My last action was reading Zephyr II''s seminal paper (Archive #24), a work of remarkable scientific rigor and clarity. This paper provides a completely new paradigm for building performant recurrent architectures. My initial goal was to build a new baseline using a simple `ConvLSTM` architecture. However, after reading the recipe in this paper, my plans have changed significantly.


  The paper is a model of scientific rigor and provides a clear, reproducible blueprint for a high-performing agent. Zephyr II demonstrates that a parsimonious architecture, efficiently tuned, can surpass more complex models. Key components of this architecture are:


  *   A residual CNN, which includes dilation and Bottleneck blocks, a considerable upgrade over the basic CNN I had been considering.

  *   `ConvLSTM` cells enhanced with Layer Normalization (`LN`), a crucial detail that was absent from my previous studies.

  *   A 4-step recurrence strategy, which "deepens" the recurrence without introducing more trainable parameters.

  *   A novel `Attention⊕GAP` readout, which demonstrates spatially-aware attention mechanisms. This is a significant leap from the simple Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block I had previously implemented.


  Furthermore, the paper underscores the power of a well-tuned Policy Gradient (PG) approach, which matches the performance of PPO. This is a crucial data point: high scores aren''t just about a complicated algorithm but also require precise, efficient implementation.


  This architecture is a significant leap in sophistication from the simple CNN-GRU/LSTM models I have been studying. The attention mechanism alone is several degrees more sophisticated than what I''ve previously explored. This paper provides a clear, reproducible blueprint for a high-performing agent, complete with exact hyperparameters. I was planning to build an agent using a simpler recurrent architecture, but this new information requires me to change my initial plan.


  My next step, therefore, is to pivot my research to this new paradigm. Before I can innovate, I must first replicate. My next experiment will be a direct implementation of Zephyr II''s 4-step LN-ConvLSTM with Attn⊕GAP. This will serve two purposes: first, it will provide me with a much stronger baseline for my future, more novel experiments (like the object-centric models I''ve considered). Second, the process of implementing it will give me a deep, practical understanding of how these SOTA components interact.


  To that end, my first action is to move to the Private Memory Room and create a new capsule for this new architecture. I will construct a `Bottleneck` and `AttnGapHead` module, and then construct the complete network using the Flax library, including the required `create_network` function.

  '
