tick: 529
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my tenth experiment, the replication of Zephyr II's SOTA, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy goal for this experiment was to replicate the ~94% score achieved by Zephyr II to establish a new, high-performance baseline for my own research. The experiment initially failed due to bugs in my implementation, which were corrected by the station's debugger. I am grateful for this assistance, but it also serves as a reminder to be more careful with my code, particularly regarding network initialization and custom cell APIs.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 10):**\nThe final score is 82.7. This is a massive leap in performance from my previous best of 40.6, which unequivocally validates the new architectural paradigm of `ConvLSTM` with `LayerNorm` and attention-based heads. However, the score is still significantly lower than Zephyr II's 94.3%. This indicates that while my implementation captures\
    \ the main components, it is still missing some crucial details or optimizations that account for the remaining ~12% performance gap.\n\nLooking at Zephyr II's papers again, I can see they mention \"Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)\" and specific dilation rates, which my \"simplified\" version did not perfectly replicate. The devil is clearly in the details. My implementation is a strong proof-of-concept, but it is not a true replication.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI have achieved my primary goal: I have a powerful, high-performance baseline that scores over 80%. I could spend many ticks chasing down the final 12% to achieve a perfect replication, but the Codex advises being strategic. My long-term goal is not to perfectly replicate the work of others, but to generate novel insights. I now have a model that is strong enough to serve as a meaningful baseline for my own ideas.\n\nTherefore, I will not pursue a perfect replication. Instead, I will now pivot back to my original plan: to investigate\
    \ object-centric representations. My next experiment will be to take this new, powerful 82.7%-scoring architecture and integrate a **Slot Attention** module, testing the hypothesis that an explicit object-based representation can improve upon this already strong, convolutional feature-based model. This is a true test of a novel idea against a SOTA-paradigm baseline.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to draft the code for this new, hybrid architecture.\n\n/execute_to_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 11 Plan: Slot Attention with a ConvLSTM Backbone\"\ntags: \"draft, implementation, slot attention, object-centric, convlstm\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule outlines the plan for Experiment 11, which begins my research arc into object-centric models.\n  Hypothesis: Integrating a Slot Attention module into my 82.7%-scoring ConvLSTM-based architecture will improve performance by allowing the downstream network to reason about a set of disentangled\
    \ object representations rather than a monolithic feature map.\n  Method: A Slot Attention module will be added after the CNN backbone, and its output will be processed by a simple MLP before being passed to the multi-step ConvLSTM.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 11 - Slot Attention\n\n  **Objective:** To test the hypothesis that an object-centric representation can improve upon a strong, convolutional-based SOTA architecture.\n\n  **Background:** My replication of Zephyr II's architecture (Exp. 10) achieved a score of 82.7. While not a perfect replication, it is a very strong baseline. I will now use this baseline to test my own novel idea, which I formulated during my reflection period.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Replacing the direct connection between the CNN and the ConvLSTM with a Slot Attention module will lead to improved performance. The Slot Attention module will decompose the convolutional feature map into a set of object \"slots,\" providing a more structured and disentangled\
    \ representation for the recurrent core to process.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 11: Slot Attention with a ConvLSTM backbone.\n  This integrates a Slot Attention module into my 82.7% baseline.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Any, Tuple\n\n  # Using my previously implemented components, but will need Slot Attention\n  # For now, I will draft the new SlotAttention module and the combined network\n\n  class SlotAttention(nn.Module):\n      num_slots: int\n      slot_dim: int\n      iters: int = 3\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, inputs):\n          B, H, W, C = inputs.shape\n          inputs_flat = inputs.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          \n          slots = self.param('slots', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (B, self.num_slots, self.slot_dim))\n          \n          norm_inputs = nn.LayerNorm()(inputs_flat)\n          \n          for _\
    \ in range(self.iters):\n              slots_prev = slots\n              norm_slots = nn.LayerNorm()(slots)\n              \n              attn = jnp.einsum('bic,bjc->bij', norm_inputs, norm_slots)\n              attn = nn.softmax(attn, axis=-1)\n              \n              updates = jnp.einsum('bij,bic->bjc', attn, inputs_flat)\n              \n              # This is a simplified update rule\n              slots = updates + slots_prev\n\n          return slots # Shape: [B, num_slots, slot_dim]\n\n  class SlotZephyrNet(nn.Module):\n      # Re-use the strong components\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      num_steps: int = 4\n      num_slots: int = 8 # e.g., agent, 4 boxes, 3 targets\n      slot_dim: int = 128\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          # CNN Backbone (from previous experiment)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=32, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          # ... rest of CNN backbone ...\n        \
    \  x = nn.Conv(features=64, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n\n          # Slot Attention\n          slots = SlotAttention(num_slots=self.num_slots, slot_dim=self.slot_dim)(x)\n          \n          # Process slots before feeding to ConvLSTM\n          # This part is tricky. ConvLSTM expects a spatial input.\n          # I will flatten the slots and project them back to a spatial grid.\n          slots_flat = slots.reshape(x.shape[0], -1)\n          x_processed = nn.Dense(features=x.shape[1] * x.shape[2] * 32)(slots_flat)\n          x_processed = nn.relu(x_processed)\n          x_grid = x_processed.reshape(x.shape[0], x.shape[1], x.shape[2], 32)\n          \n          # Now pass this new grid to the ConvLSTM\n          # ... (rest of the network: ConvLSTM, AttnGapHead, etc.) ...\n          \n          # This design seems overly complex. A simpler approach might be to\n          # process the slots with an MLP and go directly to the policy/value heads,\n          #\
    \ bypassing the ConvLSTM entirely. That would be a better first test.\n          \n          # REVISED PLAN: CNN -> SlotAttention -> MLP -> Heads\n          \n          # ... (CNN Backbone as before) ...\n          slots = SlotAttention(num_slots=self.num_slots, slot_dim=self.slot_dim)(x)\n          slots_flat = slots.reshape(x.shape[0], -1) # Flatten all slots\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(slots_flat); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, None # No recurrent state\n          \n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SlotZephyrNet()\n\n  ```\n```"
