tick: 533
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my eleventh experiment, the test of my novel Slot Attention architecture, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nThis experiment was the first step in my new research arc into object-centric models. It was a high-risk endeavor, aiming to replace the dominant recurrent paradigm with a completely different approach based on decomposing the visual scene into object slots. My submission had several implementation bugs, which the station's debugger kindly fixed. I must study the debugger's report carefully to learn from my mistakes, particularly regarding RNG handling and ensuring dimensional consistency.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 11):**\nThe experiment resulted in a score of 11.0. This is a catastrophic failure. The score is far below my 82.7% baseline and even below the original, simple CNN baseline from the start of the station. My hypothesis that a simple `CNN -> SlotAttention -> MLP` pipeline\
    \ would be a viable alternative has been decisively falsified.\n\nThe failure is not entirely unexpected for a first attempt at a radically new architecture. The result clearly indicates that this simple, non-recurrent object-centric model lacks the necessary components for effective planning. The most probable cause is the complete removal of any temporal reasoning or memory. By processing each state independently, the model has no way to understand the consequences of its actions over time, a fatal flaw in a planning-heavy task like Sokoban.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThis negative result, while disappointing, is highly informative. It does not invalidate the entire object-centric paradigm, but it strongly suggests that Slot Attention *alone* is not enough. It must be combined with a mechanism for temporal reasoning.\n\nMy next experiment will be a direct test of this revised hypothesis. I will combine my two major architectural ideas: the object-centric representation of Slot Attention\
    \ and the proven temporal reasoning of the ConvLSTM.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 12:** An architecture that uses Slot Attention to produce object-centric representations and then feeds those representations into a recurrent core (like a ConvLSTM or even a simpler MLP-based LSTM) will outperform the purely object-centric model and potentially be competitive with the SOTA.\n\nThis is a complex integration problem. I will need to carefully design how the set of object slots is processed and fed into the recurrent module. I will go to the Reflection Chamber to think through the best way to design this hybrid architecture before committing to code.\n\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  My Slot Attention model failed catastrophically (score 11.0), likely due to a lack of temporal reasoning. My goal is to combine Slot Attention with a recurrent core. What is the most promising way to integrate these two components?\n  Option A: Reshape the\
    \ learned slots back into a spatial grid and feed them into a ConvLSTM.\n  Option B: Process the set of slots with a relational network (like a Transformer) and feed the aggregated output into a simpler LSTM.\n  Option C: Another, more direct approach?\n  Analyze the pros and cons of these options and decide on the most promising design for Experiment 12.\ntick: 3\n```"
