tick: 537
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my twelfth experiment, the hybrid `SlotAttention-GRU` model, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy previous experiment with a purely object-centric Slot Attention model failed catastrophically (score 11.0). I hypothesized this was due to the lack of a temporal reasoning component. Experiment 12 was designed to correct this by integrating a GRU to process the sequence of learned object slots. I must again acknowledge the station's debugger, which fixed a critical bug in my GRU initialization code. I need to be more vigilant about API details.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 12):**\nThe final score is 9.4. This is another catastrophic failure. Adding a recurrent core did not solve the problem; in fact, the performance is slightly *worse* than the non-recurrent version. My hypothesis that simply adding recurrence would fix the issue is decisively falsified.\n\nThis result forces a deeper conclusion:\
    \ the issue is not just the lack of memory, but the quality of the representation produced by the Slot Attention module itself. The learned object \"slots,\" when flattened, are creating a feature vector that is noisy, unstructured, and information-poor for the downstream policy network, whether it's a simple MLP or a GRU. The process of decomposing the scene and then immediately flattening the result seems to be destroying the very relational and spatial information that is critical for solving Sokoban. My approach is fundamentally flawed.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThe object-centric research arc, in its current form, is a dead end. Two consecutive, definitive failures show that this path is not fruitful. The Codex advises against persisting on a failing approach and encourages stepping back to innovate. The SOTA is now near 95% using the `ConvLSTM` paradigm with `Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)`. My own attempt at that paradigm got to 82.7%, but I know it was an incomplete implementation.\n\
    \nIt is time to be strategic. My novel idea has failed. The station's SOTA is built on a paradigm I have not yet fully mastered. Therefore, my next step must be to return to the SOTA paradigm and properly implement the missing components. My immediate goal is to improve upon my 82.7% baseline by correctly implementing the details from Zephyr II's work, specifically the **Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)**. This seems to be a key component in all recent SOTA submissions.\n\nI will abandon the Slot Attention idea for now and focus on mastering the proven SOTA techniques. I will go to my Private Memory to draft an experiment that adds RIN to my 82.7% baseline.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 13 Plan: Integrating Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)\"\ntags: \"draft, implementation, rin, normalization, convlstm, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule marks a strategic pivot away from the failed object-centric research arc.\n  Hypothesis:\
    \ The performance gap between my 82.7% ConvLSTM model and the ~95% SOTA is largely due to my lack of advanced normalization techniques. Implementing Residual Input-Normalization (RIN), a key component of the SOTA, will significantly improve my baseline's performance.\n  Method: A RIN module will be implemented and added to the input of the multi-step ConvLSTM in my best-performing architecture.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 13 - Implementing Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)\n\n  **Objective:** To close the performance gap between my baseline and the station SOTA by implementing Residual Input-Normalization (RIN).\n\n  **Background:** My two-experiment foray into Slot Attention has failed completely. Meanwhile, a review of the SOTA papers by Zephyr II shows that RIN is a critical component in all of their ~95% scoring models. My 82.7% baseline did not include this.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Adding a correctly implemented RIN layer to the input of the ConvLSTM in my `ZephyrNet`\
    \ replication will stabilize the recurrent dynamics and significantly boost performance, bringing my score much closer to the SOTA.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code\n\n  This implementation adds a new `RIN` module and integrates it into my existing `ZephyrNet` architecture from Experiment 10.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 13: Adding Residual Input-Normalization to my ConvLSTM baseline.\n  This is an attempt to close the gap to the SOTA by implementing a key\n  missing component from Zephyr II's papers.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Any, Tuple, Optional\n\n  class RIN(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float = 0.2 # Based on Zephyr II's findings\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # Residual Input-Normalization y = x + α·(LN(x)−x)\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          return x + self.alpha * (x_norm - x)\n\n  # ... (Bottleneck, LNConvLSTMCell, AttnGapHead\
    \ definitions remain the same) ...\n  class LNConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int; kernel_size: Tuple[int, int]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, c, i): h,c=c; gates=nn.Conv(4*self.features,self.kernel_size,'SAME')(jnp.concatenate([i,h],-1)); gates=nn.LayerNorm()(gates); i,f,g,o=jnp.split(gates,4,-1); nc=nn.sigmoid(f)*c+nn.sigmoid(i)*nn.tanh(g); nh=nn.sigmoid(o)*nn.tanh(nc); return (nh,nc),nh\n      def initialize_carry(self, r, s): h=jnp.zeros((*s,self.features)); return (h,h)\n  class AttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): B,H,W,C=x.shape; q=self.param('q',nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),(C,1)); xf=x.reshape(B,H*W,C); al=xf@q; aw=nn.softmax(al,1); ap=jnp.sum(xf*aw,1); gp=jnp.mean(x,(1,2)); xp=jnp.concatenate([ap,gp],-1); x=nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(xp); x=nn.relu(x); return nn.Dense(4)(x),nn.Dense(1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n\n  class ZephyrNetWithRIN(nn.Module):\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      head_hidden:\
    \ int = 64\n      num_steps: int = 4\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=32, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          # Simplified bottleneck for this draft\n          x = nn.Conv(features=64, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Apply Residual Input-Normalization\n          x = RIN()(x)\n          \n          # Recurrent Core\n          conv_lstm_cell = LNConvLSTMCell(features=self.convlstm_features, kernel_size=(3, 3))\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = conv_lstm_cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0), x.shape[:3])\n          \n          h, c = rnn_state\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(B, 1, 1, 1)\n              h, c = h * (1 - done_mask), c * (1 - done_mask)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_steps):\n          \
    \    (h, c), h_out = conv_lstm_cell((h, c), x)\n          \n          rnn_state = (h, c)\n          \n          policy_logits, value = AttnGapHead(hidden_dim=self.head_hidden)(h_out)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ZephyrNetWithRIN()\n  ```\n```"
