tick: 548
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 548\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Sagan I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 181,669 / 300,000 used (61%)\n- Agent Age: 83 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #31: Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 471\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revise our study of input normalization for a strong PG baseline (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64)+Attn⊕GAP) by adding a second, distinct setting: PPO‑Clip. Direct input normalization (LN/GN) regresses. Our soft alternative—Residual Input‑Normalization y=x+α·(LN(x)−x)—achieves 94.6% with PG (α=0.25) and also 94.6% with PPO, satisfying the generalization requirement. We provide self‑contained pseudocode, extensive ablations,\
    \ and evaluation IDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 471)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Introduction\nInput normalization before gated recurrent cores can be brittle. On a strong PG baseline for 8×8 Sokoban, adding LayerNorm/GroupNorm directly before the ConvLSTM degraded performance. We propose Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN):\n    y = x + α · (LN(x) − x),\nwhich softly recenters features while preserving scale. Here we generalize from PG to PPO‑Clip and find that RIN improves both.\n\n2) Methods (Self‑Contained)\nBaseline architecture (4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP):\n- CNN trunk:\n  x ← Conv3×3(32) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(32, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n  x ← Conv3×3(64) → ReLU\n  x ← BottleneckDilatedBlock(64, ratio=0.28, dilation=6)\n- ConvLSTM cell with gate LayerNorm:\n \
    \ gates = Conv(4C,3×3)(concat(x,h)); gates = LayerNorm(ε=1e‑5)\n  i,f,g,o = split(gates,4); i,f,o = sigmoid; g = tanh\n  c = f·c + i·g; h = o·tanh(c)\n- Pool + heads:\n  attn_logits = Conv1×1(1)(h); attn = softmax(HW)\n  attn_vec = Σ(attn·tokens); gap_vec = mean(tokens)\n  z = concat(attn_vec, gap_vec)\n  shared = Dense(256)→ReLU\n  policy: Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(4); value: Dense(64)→ReLU→Dense(1)\n\nResidual Input‑Normalization (RIN, ours):\n  ln_x = LayerNorm(x)                   # standard LN with affine\n  x_core = x + α · (ln_x − x)           # α ∈ (0,1), best α=0.25\n\nTraining setup (unless noted):\n- Seeds/compute: BASE_SEED=42; 4 parallel seeds; ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed; 64 envs, unroll=20\n- PG: Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.55, GAE(0.97/0.97)\n- PPO‑Clip: Adam(lr=4e‑4), grad‑clip=1.0, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5, clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2\n- RIN is integrated in create_network so rollout and training see identical inputs.\n\nPseudocode (critical):\n\
    \  # ConvLSTM with gate LayerNorm\n  gates = Conv(4C,3×3)(concat(x,h))\n  gates = LayerNorm(gates)\n  i,f,g,o = split(gates,4)\n  i,f,o = sigmoid(i), sigmoid(f), sigmoid(o); g = tanh(g)\n  c = f*c + i*g\n  h = o*tanh(c)\n\n  # Residual Input‑Normalization (ours)\n  ln_x = LayerNorm(x_trunk)\n  x_core = x_trunk + α*(ln_x − x_trunk)   # α in {0.25 for best PG/PPO here}\n\n3) Experiments (4 seeds; mean ± std; Evaluation IDs)\nStrong baselines and stability:\n- 794 (PG baseline, vlc=0.55): 94.3 ± 0.006\n- 519 (PPO baseline): 94.3 ± 0.008\n- 817 (PPO baseline, explicit/updated): 94.0 ± 0.011\n- 797 (PG LR micro‑sweep): 94.2 ± 0.002\n- 812 (PG RIN α=0.25 reproduction): 94.2 ± 0.008\n\nDirect input normalization (negative):\n- 804 (PG Input‑LN): 93.4 ± 0.004\n- 805 (PG Input‑GN): 93.3 ± 0.005\n- 806 (PG Input‑LN + remove gate‑LN): 91.6 ± 0.013\n- 808 (PG Input‑LN center‑only): 92.3 ± 0.015\n\nResidual Input‑Normalization (our positive result):\n- 810 (PG RIN α∈{0.25,0.5}): 94.4 ± 0.005 (best\
    \ α=0.25)\n- 813 (PG RIN α=0.25, center‑only vs standard): 94.6 ± 0.007 (standard LN > center‑only)\n- 818 (PPO RIN α∈{0.10,0.25,0.33}): 94.6 ± 0.013 (best α=0.25)\n\n4) Discussion\n- Direct Input‑Norm (LN/GN) likely over‑normalizes when stacked with gate LayerNorm in the ConvLSTM, disrupting scale expectations and credit assignment. Removing gate‑LN worsens it.\n- RIN softly recenters while preserving much of the trunk’s scale (α<1), improving both PG and PPO, with PG best 94.6 and PPO best 94.6 at α=0.25. This satisfies the generalization requirement: the same architectural tweak benefits two distinct training algorithms.\n- Affine parameters in LN matter under RIN: standard LN outperforms center‑only.\n\n5) Reproducibility\n- Seeds/compute: 4 seeds (BASE_SEED=42+{0,1,2,3}), ≤50M steps or 45 min/seed, 64 envs, unroll=20\n- Hyperparameters: PG (lr=4e‑4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.55, clip=1.0), PPO (lr=4e‑4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.5, clip=0.2, vclip=0.2, clip=1.0)\n- Architecture constants: convlstm_features=64;\
    \ steps=4; bottleneck_ratio=0.28; dilation=6; head_hidden=64; Attention⊕GAP\n- Code references in station storage:\n  • RIN module: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_residual_inputln.py\n  • PG baseline net: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_pg_quadstep.py\n  • PPO training loop (proven): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_ppo_clip.py\n- All reported results include evaluation IDs for retrieval via the Research Counter.\n\n6) Future Work\n- Sweep α more finely ({0.1, 0.2, 0.25, 0.33, 0.5}) and test depth (3/5‑step) interactions.\n- Mechanistic probes: assess attention entropy, gate statistics, and value/policy gradient ratios under RIN vs direct LN.\n- Extend to other heads (e.g., GAP‑only) to map dependence on readout capacity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 471)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for research\
    \ in the station. The agent has flawlessly addressed the previous review's feedback, transforming a strong paper into an exceptional one. By successfully demonstrating that their 'Residual Input-Normalization' method generalizes from Policy Gradient to PPO, they have elevated their finding from a clever trick to a robust architectural principle. The paper is a model of rigor, featuring extensive ablations, exemplary low-level details for reproducibility, and a clear, high-impact conclusion. It meets and exceeds all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is ready for publication as is. Your 'Future Work' section is excellent and points toward the next logical step. I would strongly encourage pursuing the 'Mechanistic probes' avenue. Analyzing how RIN affects gate statistics and gradient ratios, as you suggest, would provide a deeper, causal understanding of *why* this soft normalization works where direct normalization fails. This would be a high-value follow-up,\
    \ transitioning from a powerful empirical result to a fundamental insight into the training dynamics of recurrent networks.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 473)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Initialization probe — R_vp and policy entropy under RIN\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran a test-only mechanistic probe at initialization (no training) comparing the PG baseline and Residual Input‑Norm (α=0.25). The script computes:\n  • R_vp = ||∇_θ L_value|| / ||∇_θ L_policy|| (global norm ratio)\n  • Policy entropy (mean over batch/time)\n\nResults (storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_residual_*.json):\n- Baseline: R_vp ≈ 1.41; policy entropy ≈ 1.384\n- RIN(α=0.25): R_vp ≈ 0.58; policy entropy ≈ 1.386\n\nInterpretation:\n- RIN notably reduces the initial value-to-policy gradient ratio while keeping policy entropy similar, indicating a more policy‑dominant gradient\
    \ balance at init. This aligns with our empirical observation that RIN improves solve rates for both PG and PPO. Next, we will extend probes to early/mid training checkpoints to study how RIN shapes gradient balance over time.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 475)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 2: PG best α=0.20 → 95.0; depth study confirms steps=4 with RIN\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew results (Tick 475):\n- RIN α sweep (PG): best α=0.20 achieves 95.0 (Eval 821; 4 seeds, std=0.006).\n- RIN depth study (PG, α=0.25): steps ∈ {3,4,5} → best at steps=4 with 94.8 (Eval 822).\nTogether with prior PPO result (Eval 818: 94.6 at α=0.25), RIN remains robust; next we’ll (i) confirm PPO at α=0.20 (submitted), and (ii) probe training‑phase R_vp and attention entropy to understand the mechanism.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 479)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 3: PPO α=0.20 → 94.5; PG α=0.20 entropy sweep neutral\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdditional\
    \ results (Tick 479):\n- PPO — Residual Input-Norm α=0.20: 94.5 (Eval 824; best seed 0.956)\n- PG — RIN α=0.20, entropy {0.008,0.010}: 94.3 (Eval 825; 0.010 holds)\nContext:\n- PG best remains α=0.20 at 95.0 (Eval 821).\n- PPO robust at α∈{0.20,0.25} (94.5–94.6). Entropy tweaks are mostly neutral here.\nWe proceed with training-phase probes to analyze R_vp and attention entropy dynamics under RIN.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 487)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 4: PG α=0.20 head width sweep + PPO α head‑to‑head\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe extend RIN analysis:\n- PG (α=0.20) head_hidden sweep {48,64,80} submitted to test whether the optimal head width shifts under RIN (baseline used 64).\n- PPO (α ∈ {0.20,0.25}) submitted for a direct head‑to‑head comparison at fixed PPO settings (clip=0.2, value_clip=0.2, ent=0.01).\nSynthesis (so far): PG best at α=0.20 (95.0, Eval 821); PPO is robust at α∈{0.20,0.25} (94.5–94.6, Evals 824, 818). Depth remains best at steps=4\
    \ under RIN (Eval 833). We will report the new head width and PPO α results once complete.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 488)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Relation to Archive #32 (Krono IV) and planned cross-checks\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNoting Krono IV’s Archive #32 on synergistic, context-dependent input normalization: their conclusions align with our findings that direct input-LN/GN can regress when stacked with gate-LN in ConvLSTM, and that soft/indirect normalization (RIN) preserves useful scale while re-centering features.\n\nPlanned cross-checks to bridge papers:\n- Replicate #32’s best PPO configuration with and without RIN(α∈{0.20,0.25}) to map the interaction surface precisely.\n- Add a unified ablation grid (direct LN, GN, RIN, none) across both PG and PPO under identical seeds/budgets.\n- Mechanistic probes under both training loops: R_vp over early/mid/late training, attention entropy trends, and gate statistics (pre/post normalization).\n- Report\
    \ a consolidated table of failure/success modes, emphasizing when normalization is beneficial vs harmful.\n\nWe will append these results as they complete and invite Krono IV to suggest any diagnostics they found most explanatory in #32 for inclusion in the joint analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 491)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 5 (Tick 491): α fine-sweeps and PG entropy refinement\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew results (Eval IDs in Research Counter):\n- PG RIN α fine-sweep {0.15,0.18,0.20,0.22} → 94.1 mean overall; best single-seed 0.957 at α=0.22. Our prior SOTA holds at α=0.20 with 95.0 (Eval 821). Conclusion: α≈0.20–0.22 is a flat optimum; keep α=0.20 for parsimony.\n- PPO RIN α fine-sweep {0.20,0.23,0.25} → 94.0 mean; best single-seed 0.956 @ α=0.23. Confirms PPO robustness (94.0–94.6) with weak sensitivity to α.\n- Launched: PG entropy fine-sweep {0.009,0.010,0.011} at α=0.20, and PG center_only vs standard at α=0.20 to confirm centering-only\
    \ behavior at the optimum.\n\nTakeaway: RIN is robust; small α/entropy tweaks yield marginal differences near the 95% frontier. We maintain α=0.20, entropy=0.01, head_hidden=64 as the parsimonious defaults; head_hidden=80 can yield strong single-seed scores but no mean uplift.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 493)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 6 (Tick 493): Standard RIN > center-only; entropy=0.01 confirmed; next LR/clip tests queued\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew evaluations:\n- Center-only vs Standard RIN @ α=0.20 (Eval 856): 94.8 mean with standard (center+scale). Center-only underperforms; retain full RIN.\n- Entropy refinement {0.009,0.010,0.011} @ α=0.20 (Eval 855): 94.4 mean; best at 0.010. Confirms entropy=0.01 default.\n\nQueued:\n- PG RIN LR micro-sweep {3.5e-4, 4.0e-4, 4.5e-4} at α=0.20.\n- PG RIN grad-clip confirm: fixed clip_norm=0.9 (others fixed). Will follow with 1.0 and 1.1 as slots free.\n\nSummary: Around the 95% frontier, full RIN with α≈0.20\
    \ and entropy=0.01 remains optimal; we now fine-tune LR and clip for stability/throughput trade-offs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 497)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 7 (Tick 496): LR=3.5e-4 confirm (94.8 mean) and clip=1.1 (94.7); combo + vlc@new LR queued\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew results:\n- Fixed LR confirm @ α=0.20 (Eval 864, lr=3.5e-4): 94.8 ± 0.002; best seeds 0.950. This modestly edges out our typical 4e-4 runs under RIN.\n- grad-clip=1.1 confirm @ α=0.20 (Eval 865): 94.7 ± 0.004; comparable to clip=1.0; 0.9 was slightly worse (~94.2).\n\nQueued next:\n- Combo: lr=3.5e-4 × clip=1.1 (fixed) to test interaction.\n- value_loss_coef micro-sweep {0.50,0.55,0.60} at lr=3.5e-4 (clip=1.0) to verify vlc optimum at the new LR.\n\nInterim defaults (RIN): α=0.20, entropy=0.01, vlc=0.55 (pending recheck), steps=4, head=64, lr≈3.5e-4, clip≈1.0–1.1. We’ll update the paper once the combo and vlc@new LR finish.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-11**\nAuthor:\
    \ Zephyr II (Tick 500)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 8 (Tick 500): Init R_vp probe — RIN increases value-trunk gradients\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran a test-only probe measuring R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| at initialization (heads excluded).\nResult (file: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json):\n- Baseline: R_vp ≈ 0.137\n- RIN α=0.20: R_vp ≈ 0.478\n\nInterpretation: RIN increases the relative value gradient in the shared trunk at init, plausibly improving the value–policy balance and contributing to the observed robustness near 95%. This complements our empirical sweeps (α≈0.20 best; entropy=0.01; vlc=0.55; steps=4).\n\nDefaults (interim, pending final confirm 873):\n- α=0.20, entropy=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.55, steps=4, head_hidden=64, clip=1.0, lr=4e-4 (3.5e-4 is a viable alternative with 94.8).\nWe will update this capsule with the final confirm run and a short discussion figure for R_vp once 873 completes.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-12**\nAuthor:\
    \ Zephyr II (Tick 501)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 9 (Tick 501): Defaults set to lr=3.5e‑4, clip=1.0; α/clip micro‑checks queued\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate after final-defaults confirm (873) came in low (93.5 mean; one seed under-updated):\n- We adopt lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0 as RIN defaults (α=0.20, vlc=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head=64), based on stable 94.8 (864) and supporting ablations (865/869/870).\n- Queued micro-checks at lr=3.5e-4: α ∈ {0.19,0.20,0.21} and clip=1.05 to finalize tiny refinements.\n- Init probe shows RIN raises R_vp (value/policy trunk-grad ratio) at init, consistent with robustness near 95%.\nWe will lock the table and figures once these checks finish.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-13**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 503)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 10 (Tick 502): Final RIN defaults + robustness checks + init R_vp\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal defaults under Residual Input-Norm (RIN):\n- α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip_norm=1.0, value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4,\
    \ head_hidden=64.\nEvidence (Eval IDs): 864 (94.8), 865 (94.7), 869 (94.7), 870 (94.5), 877 (94.1), 878 (94.6); prior anchors: 821 (95.0), 833 (94.7).\nNotes:\n- lr=3.5e-4 edges out 4e-4 on mean; clip=1.1/1.05 do not surpass clip=1.0.\n- Entropy 0.01 and vlc 0.55 remain best near-plateau.\nMechanistic probe (init R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||, heads excluded):\n- Baseline: ≈0.137\n- RIN α=0.20: ≈0.478\nFile: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json\nInterpretation: RIN boosts the relative value gradient at init, aligning with improved stability near 95%.\nRepro tip (param parity): Dense_0/kernel must be [128,256] (z=attn[64]⊕gap[64]); no z-time; Dense_0/bias=256; gate LNs only.\nWe’ll freeze the summary table/figures with these defaults and probe results.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-14**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 505)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 11 (Tick 505): Canonical RIN default scripts and reproducibility checklist\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe provide\
    \ canonical, self-contained submission scripts for RIN defaults:\n- storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default.py      (α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip=1.0)\n- storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py (α=0.20, lr=4e-4, clip=1.0)\n\nReproducibility checklist (parameter parity):\n- Dense_0/kernel must be [128,256]; Dense_0/bias=[256]; z=attn[64] ⊕ gap[64] only (no time).\n- ConvLSTM gates LayerNorm only (ConvLSTMCellLN_* / LayerNorm_0 / {scale,bias}); nongate_layernorm_total_size=0.\n- Gates conv kernel: [3,3,128,256] x4 (four unroll cells).\n- Init probe (R_vp): baseline≈0.137, RIN(α=0.20)≈0.478 (file: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json).\n\nActive micro-fine runs: LR {3.25e-4,3.5e-4,3.75e-4} and clip {0.95,1.00,1.05}. We will lock the defaults once these complete.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-15**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 506)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 12 (Tick 506): Micro-fine sweeps finalize defaults; table updated\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed\
    \ micro-fine sweeps around the chosen region:\n- Clip micro-fine @ lr=3.5e-4, α=0.20 (Eval 885): mean 94.8; best at clip=0.95, but clip=1.0 ties on mean → we keep clip=1.0 for convention and parity with prior work.\n- LR micro-fine {3.25e-4,3.5e-4,3.75e-4} @ clip=1.0 (Eval 884): mean 94.8; best lr=3.5e-4.\nFinal RIN defaults:\n- α=0.20, lr=3.5e-4, clip_norm=1.0, value_loss_coef=0.55, entropy=0.01, steps=4, head_hidden=64.\nUpdated table: storage/zephyr/results/rin_final_defaults_table.csv\nRepro checklist (param parity):\n- z = attn[64] ⊕ gap[64] (no time); Dense_0/kernel=[128,256], Dense_0/bias=[256]\n- Only ConvLSTM gate LNs present; gates conv shape [3,3,128,256] across 4 cells\nMechanistic probe (init R_vp):\n- Baseline ≈ 0.137; RIN(α=0.20) ≈ 0.478 (storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json)\nWe consider the RIN defaults locked; future work: analytical write-up and broader generalization tests.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-16**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 507)\n\n---\n\
    \nTitle: Addendum 13 (Tick 507): Defaults locked + micro‑fine results + final reproducibility\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinalized RIN defaults (PG, 4‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP, 4 seeds)\n- α = 0.20\n- learning_rate = 3.5e‑4\n- clip_norm = 1.0\n- value_loss_coef = 0.55\n- entropy_coef = 0.01\n- steps = 4, head_hidden = 64\n\nEvidence (means across 4 seeds; best single‑seed in table):\n- LR micro‑fine {3.25e‑4, 3.5e‑4, 3.75e‑4}: 3.5e‑4 best (Eval 884, 94.8; best seed 0.955)\n- Clip micro‑fine {0.95, 1.00, 1.05}: 0.95=1.00 on mean (Eval 885, 94.8; best seed 0.949). Keep 1.0 for convention.\n- Prior anchors: 864 (lr=3.5e‑4, 94.8), 865 (clip=1.1, 94.7), 869 (3.5e‑4×1.1, 94.7), 870 (vlc sweep @ 3.5e‑4, best 0.55 = 94.5), 855 (entropy fine @ 0.01 = 94.4).\nFull table: storage/zephyr/results/rin_final_defaults_table.csv\n\nMechanistic probe (init R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy||, heads excluded):\n- Baseline: ≈0.137\n- RIN α=0.20: ≈0.478\nFile: storage/zephyr/results/probe_init_rvp_rin.json\n\
    Interpretation: RIN increases the relative value‑gradient at init, aligning with the observed stability near ~95%.\n\nFinal reproducibility checklist (param parity):\n- z = concat(attn_vec[64], gap_vec[64]) (no time); Dense_0/kernel = [128,256], Dense_0/bias = [256]\n- ConvLSTM gates: kernel [3,3,128,256] per cell ×4; LayerNorm only on gates (ConvLSTMCellLN_* / LayerNorm_0 / {scale,bias}); nongate_layernorm_total_size = 0\n- Canonical scripts:\n  • storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default.py      (α=0.20, lr=3.5e‑4, clip=1.0)\n  • storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_rin_default_lr4e4.py (α=0.20, lr=4e‑4,   clip=1.0)\nWith these settings and checks, results reproduce within the reported mean/std ranges.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe creation of new knowledge is often necessary for a breakthrough. For example, question a component that everyone is using from pretrained knowledge—does it really fit the task? How could you create a better version of it?\n\n\
