tick: 556
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my seventeenth experiment (ID 959) are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nThis experiment was a targeted ablation to test if adding a clipped value loss to my PPO implementation would improve performance. My previous PPO run scored 86.3, slightly underperforming my PG baseline of 87.0. My hypothesis was that the clipped value loss was a key missing component for stability and performance.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 17):**\nThe final score is 84.8. This is another negative result. Adding the clipped value loss did not improve performance; it caused a slight degradation from 86.3 to 84.8. My hypothesis is falsified. This is a very perplexing outcome. I have now implemented two variants of PPO on a strong, RIN-stabilized backbone, and both have underperformed a simpler Policy Gradient algorithm. The SOTA is ~95% with PPO, yet in my hands, it is a regression.\n\nThis suggests the issue is more\
    \ fundamental than a single missing component in the loss function. There must be another critical difference between my setup and the SOTA agents' setups. The possibilities are:\n1.  **Advantage Normalization:** I recall seeing a comment in Zephyr II's script about *not* using advantage normalization. My PPO attempts have used it. This is a strong candidate for the source of the discrepancy. Normalizing advantages is standard practice in many PPO implementations, but it could be that for this specific task and architecture, it is detrimental.\n2.  **Optimizer Configuration:** While I've used the same learning rate, other optimizer details like `eps` values or the specific `optax.chain` could differ.\n3.  **Multi-Epoch vs. Single-Epoch:** I have been implementing single-epoch PPO to match Zephyr II's PG compute. However, many PPO implementations are multi-epoch. It's possible that the SOTA PPO runs are actually multi-epoch, and I misinterpreted the code.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI am at\
    \ a critical impasse. I must solve this puzzle before I can move forward. The most likely culprit is advantage normalization. I will conduct one more targeted ablation study to test this.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 18:** Removing advantage normalization from my PPO `training_step` will align my implementation more closely with the SOTA PG runs and will be the key to unlocking higher performance.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to draft this next, and hopefully final, diagnostic experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 18 Plan: PPO without Advantage Normalization\"\ntags: \"draft, implementation, ppo, ablation, advantage normalization\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule plans a final diagnostic experiment to solve my PPO underperformance.\n  Hypothesis: My use of advantage normalization, a standard PPO technique but one that may be detrimental in this specific context, is the cause of the performance deficit.\n\
    \  Method: A new PPO `training_step` will be created that is identical to my best PPO so far (Exp. 17), but with the advantage normalization step removed.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 18 - Removing Advantage Normalization\n\n  **Objective:** To determine if advantage normalization is the cause of my PPO implementation's underperformance relative to Policy Gradient.\n\n  **Background:** My PPO models (86.3, 84.8) consistently score lower than my PG model (87.0). SOTA agents use PPO to achieve ~95%. A comment in Zephyr II's code suggests they may not use advantage normalization in some cases. My implementation currently does. This is the most significant remaining difference to test.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Removing the line `advantages = (advantages - advantages.mean()) / (advantages.std() + 1e-8)` from my PPO `training_step` will improve performance by better matching the training dynamics of the successful PG algorithm.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code (for Experiment 18)\n\n\
    \  This version will be identical to Experiment 17, but with a single line commented out in the `training_step`.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 18: PPO without advantage normalization.\n  A targeted ablation to diagnose PPO underperformance.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Any, Tuple, Optional\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n\n  # ... (All architecture code and _define_hyperparameters remain identical to Exp. 17) ...\n  \n  # --- PPO Training Step WITHOUT Advantage Normalization ---\n  def training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n      from jax import vmap\n      advantages = calculate_gae(batch['rewards'], batch['values'], batch['dones'], batch['final_values'])\n      returns = advantages + batch['values']\n      # advantages = (advantages - advantages.mean()) / (advantages.std() + 1e-8) # <--- REMOVED\n      \n      # ... (Rest of the training_step\
    \ function is identical to Exp. 17) ...\n      B, T = batch['observations'].shape[:2]\n\n      def forward_all(p):\n          def process_env(env_idx):\n              env_state = jax.tree.map(lambda x: jax.lax.stop_gradient(x[env_idx]), batch['initial_rnn_states'])\n              def step_fn(carry, t):\n                  obs = batch['observations'][env_idx, t]\n                  done = jnp.where(t > 0, batch['dones'][env_idx, t-1], jnp.zeros_like(batch['dones'][env_idx,0]))\n                  logits, value, new_rnn = network.apply(p, obs[None,...], jnp.array([done]), jax.tree.map(lambda y: y[None,...], carry))\n                  return jax.tree.map(lambda y: y[0], new_rnn), (logits[0], value[0])\n              _, (logits, values) = jax.lax.scan(step_fn, env_state, jnp.arange(T))\n              return logits, values\n          return vmap(process_env)(jnp.arange(B))\n\n      def loss_fn(p):\n          logits, vpred = forward_all(p)\n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(logits,\
    \ axis=-1)\n          logp = jnp.take_along_axis(log_probs_all, batch['actions'][...,None], axis=-1).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          ratios = jnp.exp(logp - batch['log_probs'])\n          adv = jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages)\n          surr1 = adv * ratios\n          surr2 = adv * jnp.clip(ratios, 1.0 - hparams['clip_eps'], 1.0 + hparams['clip_eps'])\n          policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jnp.minimum(surr1, surr2))\n\n          vclip_eps = hparams['value_clip_range']\n          vpred_clipped = batch['values'] + jnp.clip(vpred - batch['values'], -vclip_eps, vclip_eps)\n          vloss_unclipped = (vpred - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2\n          vloss_clipped = (vpred_clipped - jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)) ** 2\n          value_loss = 0.5 * jnp.mean(jnp.maximum(vloss_unclipped, vloss_clipped))\n\n          entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(jax.nn.softmax(logits, -1) * log_probs_all, -1))\n          return policy_loss + hparams['value_loss_coef'] * value_loss - hparams['entropy_coef']\
    \ * entropy\n\n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n      params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n      return params, opt_state\n  ```\n```"
