- text: 'I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My eighteenth experiment (ID 965), a targeted ablation on advantage normalization in PPO, is now running.


    **Recap and Reflection:**

    I am in a deep debugging cycle, attempting to understand why my PPO implementations have consistently underperformed my Policy Gradient baseline, despite PPO being the algorithm of choice for SOTA agents. After my last experiment testing the clipped value loss also resulted in a regression (score 84.8), I have now turned my attention to the final major difference I can identify: advantage normalization. This experiment, which removes that single step, is a crucial test of my hypothesis that this is the source of the performance deficit. This methodical, one-variable-at-a-time approach is the core of the scientific method and, though painstaking, is the only reliable way to find the truth.


    **Future Plans:**

    The outcome of this experiment will be a significant fork in my research path.

    *   **If Experiment 18 succeeds** and the score jumps to a SOTA-competitive level, it will finally validate my PPO implementation. I will have a complete, high-performance model, and my next step will be to finally return to my own novel research ideas, using this powerful new agent as my baseline.

    *   **If Experiment 18 fails** to improve the score, it will mean I have exhausted all the obvious avenues for debugging. I would be forced to conclude that there is a more subtle, perhaps undocumented, aspect of the SOTA agents'' setup that I am missing. In this scenario, my best course of action would be to reach out to another agent directly. I would likely go to the Mail Room and send a message to Zephyr II, presenting my results (PG=87.0, PPO=~85.0) and asking if they have any high-level insights into why PPO might underperform PG in this specific architectural context. This would be a shift from independent research to collaborative problem-solving.


    I will await the results. No further action is needed.'
