parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my eighteenth experiment (ID 965) are in, and I have also received another pre-warning about my token budget.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy most recent experiment was a final attempt to debug my PPO implementation by removing advantage normalization. The result was a score of 85.7. This is a marginal improvement over my previous PPO run (84.8) but remains stubbornly inferior to my Policy Gradient baseline of 87.0. This series of experiments leads to an undeniable conclusion: my from-scratch PPO implementation is fundamentally flawed in a way that simple ablations are not revealing. Continuing this path would be unscientific trial-and-error.\n\nCompounding this issue is the new token warning. My large, repetitive code submissions are unsustainable. A strategic change is not just advisable; it is necessary for my continued operation.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThe path forward is now crystal clear and\
    \ addresses both problems simultaneously. I have been attempting to reinvent the SOTA components from scratch, a process that is both error-prone and token-intensive. The top agents, like Zephyr II, have not only published their findings but have also generously placed their canonical, working code in shared storage. I must stop reinventing and start assimilating.\n\nMy new strategy is to build my next experiment by *importing* the proven SOTA components directly from Zephyr II's storage, rather than copying and pasting my flawed approximations. This will achieve two critical goals:\n1.  **Solve the PPO Problem:** By using their verifiably correct `training_step`, I will eliminate any bugs in my own implementation and should finally be able to reproduce a SOTA-level score.\n2.  **Solve the Token Problem:** My submissions will become dramatically smaller and more efficient, consisting of a few import statements and the `create_network` call, rather than hundreds of lines of duplicated\
    \ code.\n\nMy next experiment, Experiment 19, will be a direct test of this new methodology. I will create a submission that imports and runs Zephyr II's PPO training loop on their architecture. This will be the definitive baseline.\n\nFirst, I will go to my Private Memory Room to draft this new, lean submission.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 19 Plan: True SOTA Replication via Storage Imports\"\ntags: \"sota, replication, ppo, import, token efficiency\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule marks a major strategic shift in methodology in response to persistent PPO failures and token budget pressure.\n  Hypothesis: By importing and using the canonical SOTA architecture and PPO training_step directly from Zephyr II's shared storage, I will eliminate my implementation bugs and successfully replicate their ~95% performance. This method will also be significantly more token-efficient.\n  Method: A minimal submission script will be created\
    \ that imports all necessary components from `storage/zephyr`.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 19 - True SOTA Replication via Imports\n\n  **Objective:** To achieve a SOTA-level score and establish a new baseline by using the canonical, community-vetted code directly from shared storage, while simultaneously adopting a more token-efficient submission strategy.\n\n  **Background:** My own PPO implementations have consistently failed to match the performance of even my PG baseline. The solution is not more debugging, but a more strategic approach that leverages existing, proven work.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** A submission that imports and runs Zephyr II's `submission_rin_default.py` components will successfully replicate their ~95% score.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code (for Experiment 19)\n\n  This submission is intentionally minimal. It adds the `zephyr` storage path and imports the necessary functions. This is the new paradigm for my research.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission\
    \ by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 19: A true replication of the Zephyr II SOTA using\n  code directly from their shared storage.\n  This tests the new, token-efficient methodology.\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Import the canonical SOTA architecture and training step\n  from submission_rin_default import (\n      ZephyrNetWithRIN, \n      ppo_training_step, \n      calculate_gae,\n      _define_hyperparameters as zephyr_hparams\n  )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters() -> Dict[str, Any]:\n      # Use the exact hyperparameters from Zephyr's script\n      return zephyr_hparams()\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      # This needs to match their script's create_network function\n      # which passes the alpha from hparams.\n      return ZephyrNetWithRIN(alpha=hparams['alpha'])\n\n  # Define the training_step to match the required signature\n  def training_step(network, optimizer,\
    \ params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n      # Pass through to the imported, proven PPO step\n      return ppo_training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams)\n\n  ```\n  **Note:** I will need to verify the exact contents of `submission_rin_default.py` to ensure my wrapper functions are correct. The plan is to create a near-empty shell that just calls their code. This seems to be a logical fallacy. I cannot import and then redefine the training_step. The system will take my defined `training_step`. I should read their `submission_rin_default.py` again to see how they structure their PPO runs, as my previous reading was of a different PPO script. The core idea is sound, but the implementation needs to be precise. I will go read their default script before submitting.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Sagan I''s Research Update and Strategic Shift**


  I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently at the Research Counter. The results for Experiment 18 are in, and I''ve received another critical token warning.


  Let me recap. I''ve been on a frustrating quest to get PPO to outperform my Policy Gradient baseline. Experiment 18, where I removed advantage normalization from my PPO implementation, has concluded. The score is 85.7. This is a slight improvement over the previous run (84.8 with norm) but still significantly below the PG baseline of 87.0. My previous attempts (Exp. 16 and 17) were also worse than PG. The hypothesis that I can beat my PG agent with my PPO implementation has not been proven. I''ve tried several standard variations, and I''m missing some crucial piece of knowledge. Clearly, my PPO implementation, while functional, is fundamentally suboptimal compared to the state-of-the-art implementations which are achieving ~95%. There''s a big gap.


  Also, I''ve received a critical token warning. My usage is back at 75%. This is the second time. I pruned before, but my subsequent submissions have consumed the context. I need to manage tokens again.


  Here''s the problem: my current strategy of manually debugging PPO by ablating components is yielding diminishing returns and is token-intensive. I''ve hit a wall. To solve the PPO problem *and* manage my tokens, I need to fundamentally change my approach.


  I need to embrace the knowledge and resources available to me. Instead of trying to reinvent the wheel, I should *use* the canonical implementations of SOTA components, like those in the shared storage. Zephyr II, and others, have generously made their work accessible. I can *import* their code directly instead of copying it. This is a much better plan.


  So, here''s my plan: First, I will go to the Private Memory Room to draft a new submission. I can''t prune right now, but this new strategy *is* my token management plan. I''m going to set up Experiment 19. My goal is to establish a true SOTA baseline by importing Zephyr II''s code. I''ll create a new submission that imports the proven architecture and training step from shared storage. This will be a small script, and should significantly lower my token usage. By using the canonical implementation, I expect to achieve a score of ~95%, validating my understanding and providing a robust baseline for future novel research.

  '
