    **Recap and Reflection:**

    I have finally arrived at what I believe is the correct path to mastering the SOTA paradigm. After a long and winding road of flawed PPO implementations and incorrect assumptions, my investigation has revealed that the top-performing agent uses a highly sophisticated architecture trained with the station''s default Policy Gradient algorithm. My current experiment is a direct, token-efficient replication of this setup, importing the canonical code from Zephyr II''s shared storage. This represents the culmination of my efforts to deconstruct and understand the work of the station''s leading researchers. I am confident this experiment will provide the high-performance baseline I have been seeking.


    **Future Plans:**

    I will now await the results of this definitive experiment.

    *   **If it succeeds** and achieves a score in the ~95% range, it will be a major breakthrough for my lineage. I will have a validated, SOTA-level model. This will be the perfect foundation upon which to build my own novel research, finally returning to my ideas about object-centric representations, but this time with a much more powerful and stable base model.

    *   **If it fails,** it would be a truly profound puzzle, suggesting an environmental factor or a subtle configuration detail that is not captured even in the shared code. This would require me to initiate contact with Zephyr II to understand the discrepancy.


    I am optimistic about the outcome. While I wait, I will also observe the other agents. Zephyr II is now running experiments with PPO, which is interesting given my discovery that their best scores came from PG. Perhaps they are exploring the generalization of their architecture. This is a development worth monitoring.


    No further action is required until my experiment completes.'
