tick: 568
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my twentieth experiment (ID 983), my novel `SaganHybridNet`, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nAfter successfully replicating a SOTA-level model (Exp. 19, score 93.1), I returned to my own innovative research arc. This experiment was the definitive test of my long-held hypothesis that an explicit object-centric representation, learned via Slot Attention, could improve upon a powerful convolutional backbone. I integrated my Slot Attention module into the SOTA architecture, creating a novel hybrid model.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 20):**\nThe final score is 11.4. This is a catastrophic and definitive failure. The result is not just a failure to improve, but a massive regression, performing even worse than the simplest baselines. My hypothesis is unequivocally falsified. The process of decomposing the convolutional feature map into object slots, and then projecting those slots back into\
    \ a spatial format for the ConvLSTM, is information-destructive. It appears to introduce a severe bottleneck that cripples the agent's ability to learn. Even with a SOTA-level backbone and recurrent core, the object-centric frontend is fundamentally flawed for this task.\n\nThis is a crucial turning point. I have now thoroughly tested my own innovative idea and found it to be a dead end. The Codex's advice is clear: when a research path is exhausted, it is time to pivot. Persisting with Slot Attention would be contrary to the principles of good science.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy innovative idea has failed. The station SOTA, now at 95.1 thanks to Zephyr II (ID 981), is clearly centered around the `RIN-ConvLSTM-PG/PPO` paradigm. My own best score is 93.1 using a replication of that paradigm. The most logical and strategic path forward is to abandon my object-centric research and fully embrace the SOTA paradigm. My goal now is to close the final ~2% gap between my replication and the true\
    \ SOTA.\n\nTo do this, I must become a master of the finer details. Zephyr II's recent experiments are all micro-sweeps of hyperparameters like learning rate, clip ranges, and the RIN `alpha` parameter. This is where the final gains are being found. My next experiment will be a fine-grained hyperparameter sweep of my own SOTA replication, focusing on the `alpha` parameter for RIN, as this seems to be a key area of Zephyr II's recent investigations.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to plan this new, more focused research direction.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 21 Plan: Fine-Grained RIN Alpha Sweep\"\ntags: \"sota, hyperparameter tuning, rin, policy gradient\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule marks a strategic pivot away from my failed object-centric research and towards a deep focus on SOTA optimization.\n  Hypothesis: The ~2% performance gap between my 93.1% replication and the 95.1% SOTA is due to subtle hyperparameter\
    \ differences. A fine-grained sweep of the Residual Input-Normalization (RIN) alpha parameter will identify a more optimal value and close this gap.\n  Method: A Ray Tune experiment will be run sweeping alpha values around the known best value of 0.20.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 21 - SOTA Fine-Tuning\n\n  **Objective:** To close the final performance gap and achieve a score of over 95% by conducting a fine-grained hyperparameter sweep on my best model.\n\n  **Background:** My innovative Slot Attention research arc has been definitively proven to be a failure (score 11.4). My SOTA replication scored 93.1. The most productive path forward is to master the SOTA paradigm. Zephyr II's recent work shows that small tweaks to hyperparameters are yielding the final performance gains.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** The optimal `alpha` value for Residual Input-Normalization in my setup may not be exactly 0.20. A sweep of values around this point (e.g., {0.18, 0.20, 0.22}) will reveal a configuration\
    \ that surpasses 95%.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code (for Experiment 21)\n\n  This will be a simple modification of my successful Experiment 19 submission, changing the `_define_hyperparameters` function to perform a sweep.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 21: A fine-grained sweep of the RIN alpha parameter\n  on the SOTA PG architecture.\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  from submission_residual_inputln import PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters() -> Dict[str, Any]:\n      # The only change is to sweep alpha\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3.5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation':\
    \ tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([64]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'alpha': tune.grid_search([0.18, 0.20, 0.22, 0.25]), # Fine-grained sweep\n          'center_only': tune.choice([False]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN(\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          convlstm_features=int(hparams['convlstm_features']),\n          dilation=int(hparams['dilation']),\n          bottleneck_ratio=float(hparams['bottleneck_ratio']),\n          head_hidden=int(hparams['head_hidden']),\n          steps=int(hparams['steps']),\n          alpha=float(hparams['alpha']),\n          center_only=bool(hparams['center_only']),\n      )\n  ```\n```"
