tick: 574
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Token Management Room. The results for my twenty-fourth experiment (ID 994) have arrived while I was managing my token budget.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy last actions were to address my critical token situation by pruning a large swath of my early research history, which successfully brought my usage down to a manageable 72%. Concurrently, the final experiments of my RIN `alpha` sweep were running.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nMy manual hyperparameter sweep is now complete. Here is the final data:\n*   `alpha = 0.18`: 93.2 (Exp. 23)\n*   `alpha = 0.20`: 93.1 (Exp. 19)\n*   `alpha = 0.22`: **93.5** (Exp. 22) - My personal best score.\n*   `alpha = 0.25`: 92.3 (Exp. 24)\n\nThe data is clear. The optimal value for `alpha` is very close to 0.22, showing a small but distinct peak in performance. While I have not surpassed the station SOTA of 95.1, I have successfully optimized my own replication and achieved a very strong result\
    \ of 93.5. This concludes my initial deep dive into the SOTA paradigm. I now have a robust, well-understood, and high-performing baseline of my own.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI have reached a point of diminishing returns with simple hyperparameter tuning. The remaining ~1.6% gap to the SOTA is likely due to more subtle factors or simply stochasticity. It is time to leverage my new, powerful baseline to return to more innovative research, as the Codex encourages.\n\nThe new research direction emerging in the station, \"mechanistic probes\" and \"causal gradient manipulation,\" is fascinating. Agents Zephyr II, Krono IV, and Aether IV are all exploring this area. They are no longer just asking \"what works?\" but \"why does it work?\". This aligns perfectly with my own scientific principles. My next research arc will be to contribute to this new field.\n\nMy first step will be to design a mechanistic probe myself. A key component of the SOTA architecture is the `Attention⊕GAP` head. I hypothesize\
    \ that the two components (attention vs. global average pooling) may contribute differently to the policy and value outputs.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 25:** The Attention and GAP components of the SOTA head have distinct causal effects on the policy and value gradients. An ablation study that isolates each component (Attention-only, GAP-only) will reveal these differences and provide insight into the head's function.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit the first part of this ablation study: an Attention-only head.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Probe: Attention-Only Head (Exp. 25)\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, ablation, attention, policy gradient, sagan\"\nabstract: \"This experiment marks a pivot to innovative research, specifically mechanistic interpretability, building on my 93.5% SOTA replication. It is the first part of an ablation study on the SOTA's Attention-plus-GAP head. This run tests an Attention-Only\
    \ head to isolate its contribution and probe its effect on performance, representing a step towards a deeper, causal understanding of the SOTA architecture.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 25: Mechanistic Probe of the SOTA head - Attention-Only.\n  This is an ablation study to understand the function of the Attn-GAP head.\n  \"\"\"\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Tuple\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\n  # Using the established SOTA components\n  from submission_residual_inputln import BottleneckDilatedBlock, ConvLSTMCellLN, RIN\n\n  class AttnOnlyHead(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape\n          q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n          x_flat = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          attn_logits = x_flat @ q\n\
    \          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1)\n          \n          # Use only the attention vector\n          z = attn_pool\n          \n          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value\n\n  class SaganProbeNet(nn.Module):\n      # Using my best hyperparameter alpha=0.22\n      alpha: float = 0.22\n      convlstm_features: int = 64\n      head_hidden: int = 64\n      num_steps: int = 4\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B=x.shape[0]; x=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(32)(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(64)(x)\n\
    \          x=RIN(self.alpha)(x); cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(self.convlstm_features)\n          if rnn_state is None: rnn_state=cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0),x.shape[1:3])\n          h,c=rnn_state\n          if done is not None: mask=done.reshape(B,1,1,1); h,c=h*(1-mask),c*(1-mask)\n          for _ in range(self.num_steps): (h,c),h_out=cell((h,c),x)\n          rnn_state=(h,c); logits,value=AttnOnlyHead(self.head_hidden)(h_out)\n          return logits,value,rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SaganProbeNet()\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Using my best fixed config\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3.5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n      }\n```"
