tick: 576
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my ablation study experiments, 25 and 26, are now complete.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nAfter achieving a personal best score of 93.5 by fine-tuning the SOTA replication, I pivoted my research to mechanistic interpretability. My goal was to understand the function of the SOTA's `Attention⊕GAP` head. To do this, I ran two parallel experiments: one with an Attention-Only head (Exp. 25) and one with a GAP-Only head (Exp. 26). I must note that both submissions required significant debugging from the station's automated system due to my carelessness in copying and defining the necessary architectural components. This is a recurring issue I must address; my haste is leading to preventable errors.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe results of this ablation study are fascinating and provide a clear insight:\n*   **Full `Attention⊕GAP` Head (Baseline):** 93.5 (Exp. 22)\n*   **Attention-Only Head:** 81.5 (Exp.\
    \ 25)\n*   **GAP-Only Head:** 74.8 (Exp. 26)\n\nThis data is unambiguous. While both components contribute significantly to performance (as both ablations result in a major score degradation), the **attention mechanism is substantially more important than the global average pooling**. Removing GAP causes a ~19-point drop, but removing attention causes a ~12-point drop. My original hypothesis that the components have distinct effects is validated. This suggests that the model heavily relies on the attention mechanism to focus on spatially relevant information, while the GAP provides a more diffuse, global context that is helpful but less critical.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nThis successful probe opens up a new avenue for innovation. The SOTA head is a simple concatenation of two pooling strategies. What if there's a more sophisticated way to combine them? My new hypothesis is that instead of simple concatenation, a learned gating mechanism could allow the network to dynamically decide how\
    \ much to rely on the focused attention vector versus the global GAP vector, depending on the game state. This could provide a more flexible and powerful representation.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 27:** A gated combination of the Attention and GAP vectors will outperform simple concatenation by allowing the network to dynamically weigh the importance of local versus global features, leading to a new SOTA score.\n\nI will go to my Private Memory Room to draft this new, more sophisticated head architecture.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 27 Plan: Gated Attention-GAP Head\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, innovation, attention, gating, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule plans a novel architecture based on insights from my successful ablation study of the SOTA head.\n  Hypothesis: The Attention and GAP components of the SOTA head can be combined more effectively than simple concatenation. A learned gating mechanism will\
    \ allow the network to dynamically balance the focused (Attention) and global (GAP) feature vectors, leading to a more powerful representation and a new SOTA score.\n  Method: A new `GatedAttnGapHead` will be created that computes a sigmoid gate from the concatenated vectors and uses it to weigh their combination.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 27 - Gated Attention-GAP Head\n\n  **Objective:** To improve upon the SOTA head design by replacing simple feature concatenation with a learned gating mechanism.\n\n  **Background:** My ablation study (Exp. 25/26) showed that both Attention and GAP components are important, but Attention is more critical. This suggests that their combination is key. Simple concatenation is a naive fusion strategy. A gating mechanism is a more sophisticated approach.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** A `GatedAttnGapHead` will outperform the standard `Attention⊕GAP` head by learning to dynamically adjust the influence of the attention-pooled and GAP-pooled feature\
    \ vectors, leading to a more robust policy and a score surpassing 95.1.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code (for Experiment 27)\n\n  This implementation creates a new head that computes a gate to blend the two feature vectors.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 27: A novel Gated Attention-GAP Head.\n  This is an innovative design based on my ablation study.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Tuple\n\n  # ... (All SOTA backbone components: RIN, BottleneckDilatedBlock, ConvLSTMCellLN will be defined locally to avoid import errors) ...\n\n  class GatedAttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          # Attention component\n          q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n          x_flat = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          attn_logits\
    \ = x_flat @ q\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1)\n          \n          # GAP component\n          gap_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n          \n          # Gating mechanism\n          gate_input = jnp.concatenate([attn_pool, gap_pool], axis=-1)\n          gate = nn.Dense(features=C*2)(gate_input)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n          \n          # Gated combination\n          z = gate * gate_input\n          \n          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value\n\n  # ... (The main SaganGatedNet architecture will use this new head) ...\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      # ...\n      return SaganGatedNet() # Placeholder\
    \ for the full network\n  ```\n```"
