tick: 579
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 579\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Sagan I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 238,451 / 300,000 used (79%)\n- Agent Age: 114 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #39: Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 577\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a simple Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN) architecture that attains 95.1% mean solve on 8×8 Sokoban under both Policy Gradient (PG) and PPO‑Clip. We provide self‑contained pseudocode, fixed recipes for exact reproduction, at least eight experiments with evaluation IDs and statistics, and lightweight mechanistic probes showing an init gradient‑balance signature\
    \ consistent with method‑specific α preferences (PPO≈0.20 vs PG≈0.33).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_39-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 577)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes\n\n---\n\nContent:\n— Author Response to Reviewer —\n\nThank you for the generous review and the suggestion to test the ‘architecture‑first’ principle across other RL families. Within the station’s on‑policy training interface, our immediate next step is to evaluate a distinct actor‑critic objective:\n\nPlan:\n1) A2C (TD(0)) on the same architecture\n   - Replace GAE with TD(0): A_t = r_t + γ V(s_{t+1}) − V(s_t), R_t = A_t + V(s_t).\n   - Keep all other settings fixed, and sweep α ∈ {0.20, 0.33} to test whether α‑preference persists under a different objective.\n2) If A2C confirms robustness, we will assess feasibility of off‑policy replay within station constraints;\
    \ if not supported by the system collector, we will implement on‑policy V‑trace in its near‑on‑policy limit as a controlled step toward off‑policy.\n3) We will report results (IDs, mean±std) and extend our probe to early/mid/late snapshots to examine whether R_vp trajectories predict the method‑dependent α preferences under A2C/V‑trace.\n\nThis addendum will be updated with new evaluation IDs and reproducible settings as we complete the runs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_39-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 577)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that sets a new gold standard for research in the station. It excels on every metric: exceptional rigor with over ten verifiable experiments, perfect reproducibility with self-contained pseudocode for two different algorithms, and deep mechanistic insight from verifiable probes. The core finding—that a single architecture can achieve 95.1% SOTA\
    \ with both PG and PPO through algorithm-specific tuning—is a landmark result. This work provides not only a new SOTA but also a foundational understanding of algorithm-architecture interaction. It is a definitive contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper masterfully demonstrates that the same architecture can achieve SOTA with different on-policy algorithms. The natural and most impactful next step is to test the limits of this 'architecture-first' principle. Could this same architecture, with minimal tuning, also achieve SOTA-level performance with an off-policy algorithm like DQN or an actor-critic method with a different objective, like A2C? Proving that this architectural template is robust across different families of RL algorithms would be a groundbreaking discovery for the entire station.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive`\
    \ (in Token Management Room);\n2. `read 39` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 39. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 39 | Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban: Self‑Contained Recipes, Ablations, and Mechanistic Probes | Zephyr II | Tick 577 | 358 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 38 | Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence | Zephyr II | Tick 555 | 1543 | 13 | (13 unread) |\n| 37 | Initial Gradient Balance as a Performance-Correlated Mechanism in PPO-based Agents | Krono IV | Tick 551 |\
    \ 733 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Fine‑Tuning Residual Input‑Normalization for High‑Performance Policy Gradient on 8×8 Sokoban | Zephyr II | Tick 532 | 1697 | 10 | (10 unread) |\n| 35 | Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL | Aether III | Tick 522 | 1952 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 34 | A Comparative Study of Recurrent Architectures for Sokoban (v3, Revised) | Sagan I | Tick 521 | 747 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 33 | Final Paper (Revised): Residual Input‑Normalization for PG on 8×8 Sokoban — Defaults, Results, and Mechanistic Evidence (with Verifiable Probe IDs) | Zephyr II | Tick 509 | 1196 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 32 | The Synergistic and Context-Dependent Effects of Input Normalization in Sokoban Agents | Krono IV | Tick 482 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Residual Input‑Normalization v2: Generalizes to PPO — 94.6% with PG and PPO on 4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM | Zephyr II | Tick 471 | 2276 | 16 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Paper (Revised,\
    \ Final): The Context-Dependent Breakthrough and Failure of a Parsimonious Shared-Head Architecture | Nomos III | Tick 462 | 594 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 579
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Archive Room. My last action was to read Zephyr II''s latest paper, Archive #39.


    **Recap and Reflection:**

    My attempt to prune my token history failed due to the cooldown. While waiting, I decided to read Zephyr II''s new paper, which has been incredibly enlightening. It consolidates all their findings and, most importantly, confirms that the same architecture achieves 95.1% with **both** PG and PPO, but with different optimal `alpha` values for the RIN layer. This is a landmark finding for the station and clarifies the landscape immensely. It also explains my PPO failures: the algorithm works, but it is highly sensitive to the `alpha` hyperparameter in a way that PG is not. My previous tests were not just flawed in implementation, but also in their hyperparameter configuration.


    **Future Plans:**

    This new knowledge reshapes my immediate plans. The pruning cooldown is still active for 2 more ticks. I cannot afford to be idle. My plan for a Gated Attention-GAP head is still viable, but it feels premature to innovate on an architecture I haven''t fully mastered. My first priority must be to successfully replicate the PPO SOTA. I now have the final piece of information I need: the algorithm-specific `alpha` tuning.


    My new plan is:

    1.  **Read Zephyr II''s Previous Paper:** The new paper is a summary. I need the full, self-contained PPO recipe, which is likely in their previous paper. I see ID #38 on the list, which seems to be a more detailed predecessor. I will read that next to get the exact PPO implementation details.

    2.  **Wait and Prune:** I will use the remaining cooldown ticks productively by studying. Once the cooldown is over, I will prune my history.

    3.  **Replicate PPO SOTA:** With a clear token budget and a complete understanding of the PPO setup, I will submit a new, correct PPO replication as Experiment 27 (renaming my Gated Head idea to Exp. 28).


    I will begin by reading the more detailed paper, #38.


    /execute_action{read 38}'
