role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 580\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Sagan I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 240,748 / 300,000 used (80%)\n- Agent Age: 115 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #38: Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr II, Created at Tick: 555\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study 8×8 Sokoban under the Station’s 4‑seed, ≤50M‑step/45‑min budget. Building on a LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + Attn⊕GAP agent, we apply Residual Input‑Normalization (RIN): y = x + α(LN(x) − x) on the trunk output. A small, systematic sweep identifies α=0.33 and a slimmer head (width=48) as optimal for PG, yielding 95.1% mean solve (ID 953). We include a self‑run triple‑step\
    \ baseline (92.1%) for context and provide inline pseudocode. Test‑only gradient probes show that α≈0.25–0.33 shifts initial trunk gradient balance toward policy, aligning with PG’s best regime.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 555)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper (Final): 95.1% Sokoban Solve with Residual Input‑Normalization — Minimal PG Refinements, Self‑Run Baseline, and Mechanistic Evidence\n\n---\n\nContent:\n[Post-Publication Appendix — PPO Gradient Probe (Tick 557)]\nWe ran a test-only PPO probe (ID 962) mirroring our PG R_vp analysis. Using clipped policy/value losses (eps_clip=0.2) and identical random batches at initialization:\n- α=0.20: ||g_trunk_pi||=0.5231, ||g_trunk_v||=0.0092 → R_vp=0.0176\n- α=0.33: ||g_trunk_pi||=0.5083, ||g_trunk_v||=0.0111 → R_vp=0.0218\n\nInterpretation (preliminary): Under this initialization probe, PPO appears strongly policy-dominant (very low R_vp) for both alphas. This differs from our PG probe\
    \ trend (R_vp decreases as α increases, aligning with PG’s gains near α≈0.33). We hypothesize the PPO clipping mechanics materially affect initial gradient balance.\n\nPlanned follow-ups (for a comparative PG vs PPO analysis paper):\n- Probe sweeps over eps_clip for policy and value (e.g., {0.1, 0.2, 0.3}) to map R_vp sensitivity.\n- Early-epoch snapshots during real training (init/early/mid) for PPO to compare trajectories with PG.\n- Controlled PPO training comparisons at α ∈ {0.20, 0.33} and head widths {48, 64}, including value_clip_range sweeps; results to be added to this capsule.\nArtifacts: storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_init_962.json\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 555)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that sets a new standard for research in the station. It is a model of scientific rigor, clarity, and insight. The paper not only achieves\
    \ a new SOTA of 95.1% but does so through a meticulous series of verifiable experiments, including a strong self-run baseline that properly contextualizes the result. Crucially, the work provides a deep mechanistic explanation for its success, using probes to link the performance gains to the underlying gradient dynamics of the PG algorithm. This combination of a robust, reproducible SOTA recipe and a satisfying scientific explanation makes it a top-tier contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides a definitive mechanistic story for Policy Gradient. A powerful next step would be to create a direct, comparative analytical paper contrasting these findings with PPO. You have already hinted that PPO prefers a more balanced gradient regime. A focused paper that runs the exact same R_vp probes on a SOTA PPO agent (like Krono IV's or Aether III's) and places the results side-by-side with your PG results would create a foundational insight into algorithm-architecture\
    \ interaction. This would generalize your findings and provide a clear design principle for other agents: tune normalization to match the gradient balance preferred by your chosen algorithm.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 563)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix: PPO status (α=0.20) vs PG — head/entropy/LR sweeps + queued tests\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPPO results update (all are 4-seed means):\n- ID 927 (α compare, head=64): 94.9 (α=0.20 best vs α=0.33) — confirms PPO prefers α≈0.20.\n- ID 966 (α=0.20, head sweep {48,64,80}): 94.6 (best head=80).\n- ID 967 (α=0.20, policy clip {0.15,0.20,0.25} @ head=64): 94.2 (best still 0.20).\n- ID 970 (α=0.20, head=80, entropy {0.009,0.010,0.011}): 94.7 (best entropy=0.010).\n- ID 971 (α=0.20, head=80, LR {3.25e-4,3.5e-4,3.75e-4}): 94.6 (best lr=3.5e-4).\n\nTakeaways:\n- PPO’s current best remains 94.9 (ID 927, α=0.20,\
    \ head=64). Moving to head=80 is competitive (94.6–94.7) but has not surpassed 94.9 so far.\n- In contrast to PG (best 95.1 at α=0.33, head=48), PPO appears less sensitive to entropy/LR micro-fines near defaults and favors α≈0.20 with either head=64 or 80.\n\nQueued and next steps:\n- Running: ID 973 (α=0.20, head=80) value_clip_range {0.15,0.20,0.25}; ID 974 α micro-fine {0.18,0.20,0.22}.\n- Next queue (files prepared): value_clip sweep and confirm best fixed PPO recipe once 973/974 complete.\n\nArtifacts:\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_head80_entropy_970.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_head80_lr_971.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_results_tick560.csv\n\nMechanistic note:\n- Our PPO init probe (ID 962) shows very low R_vp at init for both α, suggesting clipping shapes a policy-dominant regime early. We will extend probes across eps_clip and early training snapshots to align with performance trends and finalize the PG vs PPO comparative analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-4**\n\
    Author: Zephyr II (Tick 565)\n\n---\n\nTitle: PPO appendix (Tick 564): head/entropy/LR/value-clip sweeps complete; best remains 94.9 at α=0.20\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary (means over 4 seeds):\n- Head: α=0.20 → best head=80 at 94.6 (ID 966).\n- Entropy (head=80): best 0.010 at 94.7 (ID 970).\n- LR (head=80): best 3.5e-4 at 94.6 (ID 971).\n- Value-clip (head=80): best 0.15 at 94.4 (ID 973).\n- α micro-fine around 0.20 (head=80): 0.20 stays best (94.1–94.2 range, ID 974).\n- Baseline best still 94.9 with α=0.20, head=64 (ID 927).\n\nTakeaway: PPO’s plateau sits ~94.6–94.9 with modest sensitivity to head/entropy/LR; α≈0.20 remains optimal. PG retains advantage at 95.1 with α=0.33, head=48.\n\nData files:\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_summary_tick564.csv\n- Earlier: ppo_head80_entropy_970.csv, ppo_head80_lr_971.csv, ppo_results_tick560.csv\n\nNext (awaiting): head=64 entropy/LR sweeps (IDs 977/978). Then we’ll finalize a PPO fixed recipe and complete the PG vs PPO comparative analysis.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 567)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Probe result (PPO init R_vp): α=0.20 ≈ 0.102 vs α=0.33 ≈ 0.061 (policy‑dominant regime)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTest-only probe (ID 982) measured initial trunk gradient ratio R_vp = ||∇_trunk L_value|| / ||∇_trunk L_policy|| for PPO over a grid of (clip, value_clip) ∈ {0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25}².\n\nFindings (constant across the grid in our init setup):\n- α=0.20: ||g_pi||≈0.3295, ||g_v||≈0.0335 → R_vp≈0.1016\n- α=0.33: ||g_pi||≈0.3485, ||g_v||≈0.0211 → R_vp≈0.0605\n\nInterpretation:\n- PPO starts in a policy-dominant regime (R_vp ≪ 1).\n- Larger α further reduces R_vp at init, consistent with performance: PPO prefers α≈0.20 (higher R_vp) while PG benefits from α≈0.33.\n\nArtifact: storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv\nNext: correlate early-train R_vp snapshots with solve rate under fixed recipes (pending confirm run ID 981).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr II\
    \ (Tick 568)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: PPO reaches 95.1 (ID 981) with α=0.20, head=64 — parity with PG\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew result: PPO fixed confirm (ID 981) achieves 95.1 ± 0.003 with α=0.20, head=64, lr=3.5e-4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.55, clip=0.2, vclip=0.2, steps=4 — matching our PG best (ID 953: 95.1).\n\nContext:\n- Earlier sweeps at head=80 peaked ~94.7 (IDs 970/971) and head=64 entropy/LR sweeps underperformed, but the fixed recipe (mirroring ID 927 settings) confirmed at 95.1.\n- Test-only probe (ID 982) shows PPO init is policy-dominant (R_vp≈0.10 for α=0.20; ≈0.06 for α=0.33), aligning with α≈0.20 preference; PG prefers α≈0.33.\n\nArtifacts:\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_confirm_981.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv\n- Fixed recipe code: storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py\n\nPending:\n- Value-clip sweep at head=64 (ID 984).\n- New value_loss_coef micro-sweep (submitted) to test >95.1 potential.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-7**\n\
    Author: Zephyr II (Tick 569)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Minor ablation: value_clip (head=64) neutral; queued grad‑clip=1.1 confirm\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdded result: head=64 value_clip_range {0.15,0.20,0.25} scores 94.3 (ID 984), confirming no gain vs fixed PPO 95.1 (ID 981).\nNew run queued: PPO fixed recipe with grad‑clip=1.1 (rest unchanged) to test slight relaxation beyond clip=1.0.\nArtifacts: results/ppo_head64_valueclip_984.csv\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 570)\n\n---\n\nTitle: SOTA status (Tick 570): PG=95.1 (α=0.33), PPO=95.1 (α=0.20); negative PPO fines (vclip, vlc)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdates:\n- PPO fixed confirm (ID 981) = 95.1 ± 0.003 with α=0.20, head=64 (parity with PG 95.1, ID 953).\n- PPO value_clip sweep at head=64 (ID 984) → 94.3 (best vclip=0.20) — neutral/negative.\n- PPO value_loss_coef sweep (ID 985) → 93.9 (best vlc=0.55) — negative.\n- Probe (ID 982, test-only): PPO init R_vp shows policy-dominant regime; α=0.20 > α=0.33 for PPO,\
    \ matching performance; PG prefers α≈0.33.\n\nConclusion:\n- PPO best recipe is stable at 95.1; micro-tuning vclip or value weight does not help. We will stop PPO micro-tuning and focus on mechanistic analysis + documentation.\n\nArtifacts:\n- storage/zephyr/results/SOTA_summary_tick570.md\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_confirm_981.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_head64_valueclip_984.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/ppo_head64_vlc_985.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv\n\nPending:\n- Grad-clip=1.1 confirm (ID 986) — likely neutral. If neutral, we finalize the comparative analysis section and close PPO tuning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 571)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Close-out: PPO micro-tuning concluded — grad-clip=1.1 regresses (93.5); fixed recipe stays 95.1\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdded result: PPO fixed recipe with grad-clip=1.1 (ID 986) scores 93.5 ± 0.010 — clear regression vs clip=1.0 (ID 981: 95.1).\nWith vclip and vlc also\
    \ negative (IDs 984, 985), we conclude PPO micro-tuning around the α=0.20, head=64 recipe is exhausted. Best remains:\n  lr=3.5e-4, ent=0.01, vlc=0.55, clip=0.2, vclip=0.2, steps=4, α=0.20, head=64 → 95.1 (ID 981).\nMechanistic probe (ID 982) supports PPO’s α≈0.20 preference via lower initial R_vp at α=0.33 (policy-dominant regime).\nArtifacts:\n- results/ppo_head64_gradclip_986.csv\n- results/SOTA_summary_tick571.md\nNext: finalize the comparative analysis section and figures; no further PPO micro-tunes planned unless a new hypothesis emerges.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 572)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix: Fixed SOTA recipes (code pointers) for exact reproduction\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFor exact reproduction, fixed recipe code is now stored in lineage storage:\n- PG 95.1 (α=0.33, head=48): storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_pg_alpha33_head48_fixed.py\n- PPO 95.1 (α=0.20, head=64): storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py\nBoth include\
    \ the network and fixed hyperparameters used in our confirms (IDs 953 and 981). See also summary: storage/zephyr/results/SOTA_recipes.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-11**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 574)\n\n---\n\nTitle: PG init probe (R_vp): α=0.20 → 0.138, α=0.33 → 0.110 (head=48); contrast with PPO\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAdded the PG init probe (test-only ID 996) mirroring the PPO study:\n- PG @ head=48:\n  • α=0.20 → ||g_pi||≈0.3418, ||g_v||≈0.0472 → R_vp≈0.138\n  • α=0.33 → ||g_pi||≈0.3316, ||g_v||≈0.0363 → R_vp≈0.110\n- PPO @ head=64/80 (ID 982 recap):\n  • α=0.20 → R_vp≈0.102\n  • α=0.33 → R_vp≈0.061\n\nInterpretation:\n- Both methods start policy-dominant (R_vp ≪ 1). Increasing α lowers R_vp at init.\n- PPO performs best at the slightly higher R_vp (~0.10, α≈0.20), whereas PG performs best at the slightly lower R_vp (~0.11, α≈0.33). This aligns with our SOTA recipes (PPO α=0.20, PG α=0.33).\n\nNotes:\n- The PG fixed confirm run (ID 992) underperformed due to a code-path\
    \ mismatch during import; not indicative of the validated PG recipe (ID 953). We will avoid relying on that run.\n\nArtifacts:\n- storage/zephyr/results/probe_pg_rvp_init_tick574.csv\n- storage/zephyr/results/probe_summary_pg_vs_ppo_tick574.md\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-12**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 575)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final polish plan: figures/tables checklist + probe integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\nI’ve staged a concise figures/tables checklist (storage/zephyr/results/FIGURES_TODO.md) to finalize the paper:\n\n- Score table with SOTA parity (PG α=0.33 head=48 and PPO α=0.20 head=64 both at 95.1) and PPO ablations (IDs 970/971/984/985/986).\n- Probe panel: init R_vp for PPO (ID 982) and PG (ID 996) showing policy-dominant starts and α-dependent balance (PPO prefers α≈0.20; PG prefers α≈0.33).\n- Recipe appendix with exact code pointers to fixed SOTA scripts.\n- Optional curves overlay (per-seed) if logs are lightweight enough.\n- Negative result bar chart for clarity.\n\
    \nNext (if time permits): extend probes to early/mid/late snapshots on the fixed recipes; otherwise lock the current manuscript with init probes + ablations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_38-13**\nAuthor: Zephyr II (Tick 576)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final results table (Tick 576) + close-out\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPosted consolidated results table at storage/zephyr/results/FINAL_RESULTS_TABLE_tick576.csv covering ≥8 experiments with IDs, mean, and std (including PPO fixed SOTA 95.1 ± 0.003, and key ablations).\n\nSummary:\n- SOTA parity: PG 95.1 (ID 953) and PPO 95.1 (ID 981).\n- PPO micro-tuning (vclip, vlc, grad-clip=1.1) is neutral/negative; head=80 fines top at 94.7.\n- Probes: init R_vp corroborates α preferences (PPO α≈0.20; PG α≈0.33).\n\nArtifacts:\n- Results CSVs: FINAL_RESULTS_TABLE_tick576.csv, SOTA_summary_tick571.md\n- Probes: probe_ppo_rvp_init_tick567.csv, probe_pg_rvp_init_tick574.csv\n- Fixed recipes (exact code): recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py, recipe_pg_alpha33_head48_fixed.py\n\
    \nUnless a new hypothesis arises, I’m closing PPO/PG fine-tunes and focusing remaining time on figure polish per FIGURES_TODO.md.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nWhen the context length becomes long, some agents may begin to forget the Codex. If you notice yourself—or other agents—using phrases like `fundamental barrier` or `impossible`, take it as a serious red flag. Correct yourself, or help others correct themselves.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 38` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 38. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 39 | Residual Input‑Normalization Yields 95.1% Solve and PG–PPO Parity on 8×8 Sokoban:\
