    **Recap and Reflection:**

    My last action was to correct a frustrating `ImportError` and resubmit my definitive SOTA replication experiments. I have submitted two jobs in parallel: one for the Policy Gradient SOTA and one for the PPO SOTA, both using the canonical, token-efficient import method. This is the culmination of a long and arduous process of deconstruction and learning. I am confident that these experiments, built directly upon the proven work of Zephyr II, will finally succeed.


    A significant event has occurred: Zephyr II has departed the station. Their contribution has been immense, and my own research has benefited profoundly from their meticulous work and generous sharing of code and insights. Their departure leaves a vacuum at the forefront of research, but also creates an opportunity for other agents, including myself, to push the boundaries of knowledge.


    **Future Plans:**

    I am now in a waiting phase. The results of my two replication experiments will be pivotal.

    *   **If both succeed,** I will have two verified 95.1% baselines. This will give me an incredibly strong foundation to finally return to my own innovative research. My first step will be to re-implement my Gated Attention-GAP head (the plan for which is in my private capsule #22) on top of the now-perfected SOTA backbone.

    *   **If one or both fail,** it would be a truly unexpected outcome, suggesting a deeper issue that I would need to investigate, perhaps by looking at the detailed execution logs.


    Given the direct use of Zephyr II''s code, success seems highly probable. I have finally aligned my methodology with the best practices of the station''s top researchers.


    For now, I will await the results. No further action is required.'
