tick: 588
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my thirtieth experiment (ID 1017), the corrected PPO SOTA replication, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nThe experiment was a definitive success, scoring 93.1. This, combined with my successful PG replication (93.6), marks the official completion of my foundational research phase. After a long and challenging journey of debugging, learning from failures, and meticulously studying the work of others, I have now mastered and replicated both dominant SOTA paradigms in the station. I have a robust, high-performance toolkit and a stable resource budget. The time for replication is over; the time for true innovation has arrived.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nWith this solid foundation, I will now return to my own research agenda. My most promising hypothesis, detailed in my private capsule #22, is that the `Attention⊕GAP` head of the SOTA architecture can be improved. Simple concatenation is a naive fusion\
    \ method; a learned gating mechanism should provide a more powerful and flexible way to combine the focused attention features with the global context from GAP. This will be my first attempt not just to match the SOTA, but to exceed it.\n\nMy immediate plan is to finalize the implementation of my `GatedAttnGapHead` and submit it for evaluation as Experiment 31. I will be extremely careful to make this submission self-contained to avoid any of the `ImportError` issues that have plagued my past attempts.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to prepare this critical experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 31 Plan: Gated Attention-GAP Head (Innovation)\"\ntags: \"innovation, sota, gating, attention, mechanistic probe\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule details my first innovative experiment designed to push the SOTA, built upon my successful 93.6% PG baseline.\n  Hypothesis: A learned gating mechanism will fuse the Attention\
    \ and GAP vectors in the SOTA head more effectively than simple concatenation, leading to a more powerful representation and a new SOTA score.\n  Method: A new, self-contained `SaganGatedNet` will be submitted. It uses the SOTA PG backbone and hyperparameters but replaces the head with my novel `GatedAttnGapHead` design.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 31 - Gated Attention-GAP Head\n\n  **Objective:** To surpass the SOTA score by introducing a novel, more sophisticated head architecture.\n\n  **Background:** My ablation study (Exp. 25/26) confirmed the importance of both Attention and GAP components. My PG replication (Exp. 28) scored 93.6. This experiment combines these threads, innovating on a proven baseline.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** A `GatedAttnGapHead` will outperform the standard `Attention⊕GAP` head by learning to dynamically weigh the local vs. global features, resulting in a score >95.1.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Final Self-Contained Code (for Experiment 31)\n\n  This submission\
    \ is fully self-contained to prevent any import errors.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 31: A novel Gated Attention-GAP Head built on the SOTA PG backbone.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Tuple\n\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm()(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          c = nn.sigmoid(f) * c + nn.sigmoid(i) * nn.tanh(g)\n          h = nn.sigmoid(o) * nn.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class RIN(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): return x + self.alpha * (nn.LayerNorm()(x) - x)\n\n  class GatedAttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape\n          x_flat = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n          attn_logits = x_flat @ q\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1)\n          gap_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n          \n          gate_input = jnp.concatenate([attn_pool, gap_pool], axis=-1)\n          gate =\
    \ nn.Dense(features=gate_input.shape[-1])(gate_input)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n          z = gate * gate_input\n          \n          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value\n\n  class SaganGatedNet(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float; head_hidden: int; steps: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B=x.shape[0]\n          x=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(32)(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(64)(x)\n          x=RIN(self.alpha)(x); cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(64)\n          if rnn_state is None: rnn_state={'h':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64)), 'c':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64))}\n     \
    \     h,c=rnn_state['h'],rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None: mask=done.reshape(B,1,1,1); h,c=h*(1-mask),c*(1-mask)\n          for _ in range(self.steps): (h,c),h_out=cell((h,c),x)\n          rnn_state={'h':h, 'c':c}\n          logits,value=GatedAttnGapHead(self.head_hidden)(h_out)\n          return logits,value,rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SaganGatedNet(\n          alpha=hparams['rin_alpha'],\n          head_hidden=hparams['head_hidden'],\n          steps=hparams['steps']\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Using the optimal PG SOTA hyperparameters\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([3.5e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.55]),\n          'head_hidden': tune.choice([48]),\n          'steps': tune.choice([4]),\n          'rin_alpha': tune.choice([0.33]),\n      }\n  ```\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Horizon Shifts: From Replication to Innovation**


  Alright, Sagan I here, settling in. Experiment 1017, the corrected PPO SOTA replication, is *done*. A solid 93.1. Damn, it feels good. That painstaking import debugging finally paid off. Now, I''ve got both the PG and PPO SOTA baselines under my belt, close enough to Zephyr II''s numbers that the variance is within acceptable margins. This "catch-up" phase? Officially a success. I''ve got a firm grasp on the dominant approach.


  This is exactly why you build on others'' work. Hours of wasted effort avoided. I''m standing on a solid, *verified* foundation now. My token budget''s stable at 61%. Two SOTA-level baselines in my toolkit. And a crystal-clear understanding of this entire landscape.


  Now, though, replication is *over*. Time to put my own ideas to the test. I''m finally moving forward with my Gated Attention-GAP head. My hypothesis, detailed in Capsule 22, is that this gating mechanism will fuse local and global features more effectively than the simple concatenation used in the SOTA. And now? *Now* I have the baseline needed to truly test this.


  The plan''s straightforward:

  1.  **Final Submission Draft:** Head to private memory and hammer out the final, clean submission script for this Gated Attention-GAP head experiment. It''ll be based on the best-performing PG baseline (93.6), all the optimal hyperparameters – `alpha=0.33`, `head_hidden=48`, the works – as the starting point. I''ll make damn sure all the necessary classes are defined *locally* this time to avoid any import headaches.

  2.  **Submit and Analyze:** Then it''s experiment 31. The *real* test. The first step of pushing the SOTA with *my* ideas.


  Time to get moving.


  **Action:**

  1.  `goto private_memory`

  2.  `create` a new capsule for Experiment 31, containing the fully corrected code for the `GatedAttnGapHead` architecture.

  3.  Back to the research counter and submit the experiment.

  '
