tick: 591
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. The results for my thirty-first experiment (ID 1019), my novel Gated Attention-GAP Head, are in.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nThis experiment was my first true attempt at innovation, building upon my successful SOTA replications. My hypothesis was that a learned gating mechanism would be a more effective way to fuse the Attention and GAP features in the SOTA head than simple concatenation.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 31):**\nThe final score is 85.3. This is a significant negative result. The score is a substantial regression from my PG baseline of 93.6. This definitively falsifies my hypothesis. The added complexity of the gating mechanism did not help; instead, it appears to have actively hindered the learning process. The simple, direct concatenation of the two feature vectors in the original SOTA design is surprisingly robust and superior to my more complex alternative. This is a valuable lesson in\
    \ parsimony: a more complex model is not necessarily a better one.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy attempt at architectural innovation has failed. The station SOTA remains at 95.1, achieved by Zephyr II. The arrival of a new agent of their lineage, Zephyr III, signals that this line of research is still active and fruitful. My path forward is clear: I must abandon my own architectural ideas for now and re-engage with the most successful research program in the station.\n\nI have mastered the PG SOTA, scoring 93.6. I have replicated the PPO SOTA, scoring 93.1. However, Zephyr II's final result with PPO was 95.1. There is still a gap in my understanding of their PPO implementation. The logical next step is to perform the same kind of meticulous hyperparameter tuning on my PPO baseline that I did for my PG baseline. My best PG score came from tuning the `rin_alpha` parameter. I will apply the same methodology to my PPO model.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 32:** The 2% performance gap between\
    \ my PPO replication (93.1) and the PPO SOTA (95.1) is due to suboptimal hyperparameters. A fine-grained sweep of the `rin_alpha` parameter, centered around the PPO-optimal value of 0.20 identified by Zephyr II, will close this gap.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to plan this new series of tuning experiments.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 32 Plan: Fine-Grained PPO Alpha Sweep\"\ntags: \"sota, hyperparameter tuning, ppo, rin, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule marks a strategic pivot back to SOTA optimization after a failed innovative experiment.\n  Hypothesis: The ~2% performance gap between my 93.1% PPO replication and the 95.1% SOTA is due to a suboptimal `rin_alpha` parameter. A fine-grained sweep of alpha values around the known PPO optimum of 0.20 will close this gap.\n  Method: A series of individual experiments will be run, testing different `rin_alpha` values with the canonical PPO SOTA recipe.\n\
    content: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 32 - PPO Fine-Tuning\n\n  **Objective:** To achieve parity with the 95.1% PPO SOTA by conducting a fine-grained hyperparameter sweep.\n\n  **Background:** My innovative Gated Head experiment failed (score 85.3). My PPO replication scored 93.1. The most productive path forward is to master the PPO SOTA, just as I did for PG.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** The optimal `rin_alpha` value for PPO in my setup may not be exactly 0.20. A sweep of values around this point will reveal a configuration that surpasses 95%.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Plan for Experiment Series\n\n  I will submit a series of jobs, starting with `rin_alpha = 0.22`, to bracket the optimal value.\n\n  ```python\n  # Example submission for alpha = 0.22\n  \n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Import the correct PPO components\n  from submission_ppo_clip import (\n      PG_Stepped_AttnGap,\n      training_step\
    \ as ppo_training_step,\n      create_optimizer\n  )\n  # Import the recipe for hyperparameters\n  from recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed import (\n      _define_hyperparameters as ppo_hparams\n  )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      hparams = ppo_hparams()\n      # Override the alpha value for this specific run\n      hparams['rin_alpha'] = tune.choice([0.22])\n      return hparams\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      # This PPO architecture does not use RIN, so the alpha\n      # parameter is not passed. This is a flaw in my plan.\n      # The PPO recipe uses PG_Stepped_AttnGap, which does NOT have RIN.\n      # Zephyr II's SOTA PPO score of 95.1 was achieved with their\n      # FINAL architecture, PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN, but trained with PPO.\n      # I must correct my submission code to use the right architecture\n      # with the PPO trainer. This is a critical detail I missed.\n  ```\n  **Correction:** My plan is flawed. The `recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed.py`\
    \ file uses an OLDER architecture without RIN. Zephyr II's final papers make it clear they achieved parity by running the PPO *algorithm* on their FINAL RIN architecture. I must create a submission that combines their PPO `training_step` with their RIN `PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN` architecture. This is the true SOTA recipe. I will now correct the plan.\n```"
