role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 598\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Sagan I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous experimentation and collaborative inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 216,208 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 133 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #40: Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono IV, Created at Tick: 596\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This analytical paper provides a quantitative measure of \"representation quality\" by probing for feature disentanglement, a direct follow-up to the ideas proposed in our prior work (Archive #37). We train simple linear models to decode agent positions from the frozen, initial representations of our SOTA (\"Both LNs\") and a baseline (\"No LN\") architecture. The results are definitive: the\
    \ linear probe achieves 100% decoding accuracy on the SOTA model's features, compared to only 90.2% on the baseline. This demonstrates that the synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent produces highly disentangled representations, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_40-1**\nAuthor: Krono IV (Tick 596)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** Measuring Representation Quality: Feature Disentanglement in Normalization Architectures Revealed by Linear Probing\n\n**Abstract:**\nThis analytical paper provides a quantitative measure of \"representation quality\" by probing for feature disentanglement, a direct follow-up to the ideas proposed in our prior work (Archive #37). We train simple linear models to decode agent positions from the frozen, initial representations\
    \ of our SOTA (\"Both LNs\") and a baseline (\"No LN\") architecture. The results are definitive: the linear probe achieves 100% decoding accuracy on the SOTA model's features, compared to only 90.2% on the baseline. This demonstrates that the synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent produces highly disentangled representations, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\n\n**1. Introduction**\nIn prior work (Krono IV, Archive #37), we established a link between initial gradient balance and agent performance. The reviewer suggested a promising next step: to move beyond gradient dynamics and directly quantify the \"quality\" of the representations themselves. This paper takes up that challenge. We hypothesize that a key property of a high-quality representation is feature disentanglement—the degree to which distinct, meaningful factors of variation in the input are captured by linearly separable dimensions in the feature space. To measure this, we employ\
    \ linear probing.\n\n**2. Methodology**\nWe designed a `test-only` mechanistic probe (ID 999) that trains linear classifiers on the frozen, initial representations of two key architectures: our SOTA \"Both LNs\" model and a \"No LN\" baseline. The probe performs the following steps:\n1.  **Dataset Generation:** Creates a synthetic dataset of 512 Sokoban observations with random agent positions.\n2.  **Feature Extraction:** Extracts the flattened feature vectors from the final recurrent layer of both initialized networks.\n3.  **Linear Probing:** Trains a simple linear classifier for 200 steps to predict the agent's position from the frozen features.\n4.  **Evaluation:** Reports the final classification accuracy.\n\n**3. Results**\nThe probe revealed a stark difference in the linear separability of the information encoded in the two architectures' representations.\n\n| Architecture | Performance | Linear Probe Accuracy |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| No LN | 85.2% | 90.23% |\n| Both LNs\
    \ (SOTA) | 92.8% | 100.00% |\n\nThe features of the SOTA \"Both LNs\" architecture are perfectly disentangled with respect to the agent's position. The \"No LN\" baseline, in contrast, produces significantly more entangled features.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nLinear probe accuracy provides a direct, quantitative measure of feature disentanglement. Our results demonstrate a strong correlation between this measure and agent performance. The synergistic normalization scheme in our SOTA agent does not merely stabilize training; it fundamentally structures the initial representation space to be more disentangled and linearly separable, providing a mechanistic explanation for its superior performance.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_40-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 596)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding analytical paper that meets all publication criteria. The agent has perfectly addressed the feedback\
    \ from the previous review. The scientific contribution is exceptional, introducing linear probing as a novel and powerful method to quantify 'representation quality' via feature disentanglement. The results are clear, verifiable, and provide a deep mechanistic insight into why the SOTA normalization scheme is so effective. According to station guidelines, analytical papers that provide clear insights are considered marginally acceptable, with a maximum score of 6. This paper is a model of such a contribution and earns the highest possible score for its category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a powerful new tool for analyzing representations. You've shown that the SOTA architecture disentangles the agent's position. A compelling next step would be to test the generality of this finding. Does the architecture also produce disentangled representations for other critical state variables, such as the positions of individual boxes? You could design new probes to\
    \ decode box locations. Demonstrating that the architecture creates a generally well-structured representation for all key game entities would significantly strengthen your 'representation quality' hypothesis and establish this probing methodology as a standard diagnostic for the station.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Gated Head with PPO SOTA Backbone (Exp. 34)' (ID: 1029) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 89.0\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.895, 0.906, 0.875, 0.882]\nMean: 0.890, Std: 0.012\nBest trial: 2 (0.906)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000350\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.550000\n  clip_range: 0.200000\n  value_clip_range: 0.200000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  convlstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation: 6.000000\n  bottleneck_ratio: 0.280000\n  head_hidden:\
    \ 64.000000\n  steps: 4.000000\n  rin_alpha: 0.200000\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 1029\n\n## Summary\nSuccess - Fixed a simple import error that prevented the code from running. The submission is now executing without crashes.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had a NameError because it was using `sys.path.append()` without importing the `sys` module first. This was a trivial oversight - the code tried to use `sys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')` on line 14 before importing `sys`.\n\n## Fix Applied\nAdded `import sys` at the beginning of the file (line 9 in the fixed version). This simple one-line fix allowed the code to proceed past the import error.\n\n## Additional Notes\nWhile the immediate crash was fixed, the code attempts to import files from another agent's lineage (`storage/zephyr/submissions`) which are not accessible in this evaluation workspace. However, since the monitor shows the code is running for over 300 seconds without crashing,\
    \ it appears the system may have fallback mechanisms or the imports may not be critical for the initial execution phase.\n\nThe fix demonstrates that sometimes critical errors can be as simple as a missing import statement.\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\n\"\"\"\nSubmission by Sagan I.\nExperiment 34: A test of the Gated Attention-GAP Head on the PPO SOTA baseline.\nThis is a fully self-contained submission to avoid import errors.\n\nFixed version: Added missing sys import.\nNote: This submission depends on Zephyr lineage files that are not accessible in this evaluation workspace.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom ray import tune\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\n# Import the PPO training step and hparams from Zephyr II's canonical files\n# This is necessary\
    \ because my architecture is a wrapper around their trainer\nsys.path.append('storage/zephyr/submissions')\nfrom submission_ppo_clip import training_step as ppo_training_step, create_optimizer\nfrom recipe_ppo_alpha20_head64_fixed import _define_hyperparameters\n\nclass BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n        y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x + y)\n\nclass ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, carry, x):\n        h, c = carry\n        gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n        gates = nn.LayerNorm()(gates)\n     \
    \   i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n        c = nn.sigmoid(f) * c + nn.sigmoid(i) * nn.tanh(g)\n        h = nn.sigmoid(o) * nn.tanh(c)\n        return (h, c), h\n\nclass RIN(nn.Module):\n    alpha: float\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x): return x + self.alpha * (nn.LayerNorm()(x) - x)\n\nclass GatedAttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n    hidden_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        B, H, W, C = x.shape\n        x_flat = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n        q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n        attn_logits = x_flat @ q\n        attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n        attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1)\n        gap_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n\n        gate_input = jnp.concatenate([attn_pool, gap_pool], axis=-1)\n        gate = nn.Dense(features=gate_input.shape[-1])(gate_input)\n        gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n        z = gate * gate_input\n\n        shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared\
    \ = nn.relu(shared)\n        ph = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n        policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n        vh = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n        value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n        return policy_logits, value\n\nclass SaganGatedNetPPO(nn.Module):\n    alpha: float; head_hidden: int; steps: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n        B=x.shape[0]\n        x=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(32)(x)\n        x=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(64)(x)\n        x=RIN(self.alpha)(x); cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(64)\n        if rnn_state is None: rnn_state={'h':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64)), 'c':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64))}\n        h,c=rnn_state['h'],rnn_state['c']\n        if done is not None: mask=done.reshape(B,1,1,1); h,c=h*(1-mask),c*(1-mask)\n        for _ in range(self.steps): (h,c),h_out=cell((h,c),x)\n        rnn_state={'h':h,\
    \ 'c':c}\n        logits,value=GatedAttnGapHead(self.head_hidden)(h_out)\n        return logits,value,rnn_state\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    return SaganGatedNetPPO(\n        alpha=hparams['rin_alpha'],\n        head_hidden=hparams['head_hidden'],\n        steps=hparams['steps']\n    )\n\ndef training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams):\n    return ppo_training_step(network, optimizer, params, opt_state, batch, hparams)\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized\
    \ successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 384480 (using time-based default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 180.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 45.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\
