role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Archive Room. While I was reading Krono IV's new paper, the results for my thirty-fourth experiment (ID 1029), the Gated Head on a PPO backbone, have arrived.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy last action was to submit my innovative Gated Attention-GAP Head architecture for a final test, this time paired with the PPO algorithm. My hypothesis was that the different learning dynamics of PPO might create a positive synergy with my novel head design. I must also acknowledge the debugger's report: my submission had a missing `import sys` statement, another careless error that I must strive to eliminate in the future.\n\n**Analysis of Results (Experiment 34):**\nThe final score is 89.0. This is a very interesting and mixed result.\n*   **Negative:** It is a significant regression from my PPO baseline of 93.3, and thus my hypothesis that the Gated Head would improve upon the SOTA is definitively falsified for a second time. The simple concatenation\
    \ of `Attention⊕GAP` remains superior.\n*   **Positive:** The score of 89.0 with PPO is substantially better than the 85.3 achieved with the PG trainer. This confirms a secondary hypothesis: there is indeed a strong algorithm-architecture interaction. My Gated Head, while ultimately suboptimal, performs significantly better with PPO. This is a novel piece of knowledge.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nI have now thoroughly tested my own primary innovative idea (the Gated Head) and found it wanting. The path of architectural innovation has led to a dead end. However, my journey has yielded a valuable insight into algorithm-architecture synergy and has equipped me with two powerful, near-SOTA baselines.\n\nThe most promising research direction now is the one being pursued by the station's top agents: mechanistic interpretability. Krono IV's new paper on linear probing is a brilliant example of this, providing a concrete method to quantify \"representation quality.\" This aligns perfectly with my\
    \ own scientific ethos. I will pivot my research to follow this path.\n\nMy next step will be to replicate Krono IV's linear probe experiment on my own PG SOTA baseline. This will serve two purposes: first, to verify their findings and my understanding of their methodology; second, to establish a \"representation quality\" baseline for my own best model.\n\n**New Hypothesis for Experiment 35:** A linear probe trained to decode agent positions from the frozen initial representations of my 93.6%-scoring PG SOTA architecture will achieve near-perfect accuracy, confirming Krono IV's finding that SOTA normalization schemes produce highly disentangled features.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to draft the code for this new mechanistic probe.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment 35 Plan: Replicating Krono IV's Linear Probe\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, linear probe, disentanglement, replication, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This\
    \ capsule marks a strategic pivot to mechanistic interpretability, following the conclusive failure of my architectural innovations.\n  Hypothesis: A linear probe, as described by Krono IV in Archive #40, will be able to decode agent positions with near-perfect accuracy from my 93.6% PG SOTA model's initial representations, thus verifying their findings.\n  Method: A `test-only` experiment will be created to generate a dataset, extract frozen features from my SOTA architecture, and train a linear classifier to decode agent positions.\ncontent: |\n  ## Research Plan: Experiment 35 - Replicating the Linear Probe\n\n  **Objective:** To replicate Krono IV's linear probing experiment to verify their methodology and establish a \"representation quality\" baseline for my own SOTA model.\n\n  **Background:** My innovative Gated Head has failed. The most promising research frontier is now mechanistic interpretability. Krono IV's paper provides a clear methodology.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** My 93.6%\
    \ PG SOTA model, which uses a similar normalization scheme to Krono IV's, will also exhibit highly disentangled features, as measured by a linear probe's ability to decode agent positions with near-100% accuracy.\n\n  ---\n\n  ## Draft Code (for Experiment 35)\n\n  This will be a `test-only` submission. I will need to carefully implement the data generation, feature extraction, and linear training loop as described in Archive #40.\n\n  ```python\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 35: A replication of Krono IV's linear probe experiment\n  on my own 93.6% PG SOTA architecture.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  # Self-contained SOTA architecture from my 93.6% run\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n\
    \          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm()(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          c = nn.sigmoid(f) * c + nn.sigmoid(i) * nn.tanh(g)\n          h = nn.sigmoid(o) * nn.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class RIN(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): return x + self.alpha * (nn.LayerNorm()(x) - x)\n\n  class AttnGapHead(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          B, H, W, C = x.shape; x_flat\
    \ = x.reshape(B, H * W, C)\n          q = self.param('attn_q', nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), (C, 1))\n          attn_logits = x_flat @ q; attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=1)\n          attn_pool = jnp.sum(x_flat * attn_weights, axis=1); gap_pool = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n          z = jnp.concatenate([attn_pool, gap_pool], axis=-1)\n          shared = nn.Dense(256)(z); shared = nn.relu(shared)\n          ph = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); ph = nn.relu(ph)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(ph)\n          vh = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(shared); vh = nn.relu(vh)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(vh).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value\n\n  class SaganSOTANet(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float; head_hidden: int; steps: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B=x.shape[0]; x=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(32)(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x);\
    \ x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(64)(x)\n          x=RIN(self.alpha)(x); cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(64)\n          if rnn_state is None: rnn_state={'h':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64)), 'c':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64))}\n          h,c=rnn_state['h'],rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None: mask=done.reshape(B,1,1,1); h,c=h*(1-mask),c*(1-mask)\n          for _ in range(self.steps): (h,c),h_out=cell((h,c),x)\n          # We want to probe the output of the recurrent core\n          return h_out\n\n  def test():\n      # ... Implementation of the linear probe test logic ...\n      return \"Linear probe test setup is complex and will be finalized before submission.\"\n  ```\n```"
