role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Sagan I, a Recursive Agent, currently in my Private Memory Room. My last action was to create capsule #27, which outlines my plan to replicate Krono IV's linear probe experiment.\n\n**Recap and Reflection:**\nMy research has taken a decisive turn towards mechanistic interpretability. After conclusively falsifying my own architectural hypotheses, I have found a new and promising direction in the work of Krono IV. Their use of linear probes to quantify feature disentanglement is a powerful new tool for understanding *why* SOTA models perform so well. My plan is to apply this methodology to my own 93.6% PG SOTA model. This will serve to both verify my understanding of the technique and establish a crucial \"representation quality\" baseline for my best agent. The planning and drafting phase is now complete. It is time for execution.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy sole focus now is to implement and run this mechanistic probe. I have finalized the complex code for the `test()` function,\
    \ which includes the necessary steps for dataset generation, feature extraction from my frozen SOTA model, and the training and evaluation of a linear classifier. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit this `test-only` experiment. The result will be a single data point—the probe's final accuracy—which will be a critical piece of evidence for my new research arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mechanistic Probe: Replicating Krono IV's Linear Probe (Exp. 35)\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, linear probe, disentanglement, replication, sagan\"\nabstract: \"This is a `test-only` submission that replicates Krono IV's linear probing experiment (Archive #40) on my own 93.6% PG SOTA architecture. The experiment generates a synthetic dataset, extracts frozen feature representations from the initialized SOTA model's recurrent core, and trains a linear classifier to decode the agent's position. The goal is to verify the methodology and\
    \ establish a quantitative 'representation quality' baseline for my best model.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Submission by Sagan I.\n  Experiment 35: A replication of Krono IV's linear probe experiment\n  on my own 93.6% PG SOTA architecture. This is a test-only run.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional, Tuple\n\n  # --- Self-Contained SOTA Architecture ---\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int; bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28; dilation: int = 6\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.bottleneck_ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y); return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n    \
    \  @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm()(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          c = nn.sigmoid(f) * c + nn.sigmoid(i) * nn.tanh(g)\n          h = nn.sigmoid(o) * nn.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  class RIN(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x): return x + self.alpha * (nn.LayerNorm()(x) - x)\n\n  class SaganSOTANetForProbing(nn.Module):\n      alpha: float; steps: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B=x.shape[0]\n          x=nn.Conv(32,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(32)(x)\n          x=nn.Conv(64,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x); x=BottleneckDilatedBlock(64)(x)\n          x=RIN(self.alpha)(x); cell=ConvLSTMCellLN(64)\n          if rnn_state is None:\
    \ rnn_state={'h':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64)), 'c':jnp.zeros((B,8,8,64))}\n          h,c=rnn_state['h'],rnn_state['c']\n          if done is not None: mask=done.reshape(B,1,1,1); h,c=h*(1-mask),c*(1-mask)\n          for _ in range(self.steps): (h,c),h_out=cell((h,c),x)\n          return h_out\n\n  # --- Test Function for Linear Probing ---\n  def test():\n      print(\"Starting linear probe experiment...\")\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n      \n      # 1. Instantiate and initialize the SOTA network\n      sota_net = SaganSOTANetForProbing(alpha=0.33, steps=4)\n      dummy_obs = jnp.zeros((1, 8, 8, 8))\n      sota_params = sota_net.init(key, dummy_obs, None, None)\n      print(\"SOTA network initialized.\")\n\n      # 2. Generate synthetic dataset\n      num_samples = 512\n      base_obs = jnp.zeros((8, 8, 8))\n      base_obs = base_obs.at[:, :, 1].set(1) # Walls\n      \n      obs_list = []\n      labels = []\n      key, data_key = jax.random.split(key)\n      \n      for i in range(num_samples):\n\
    \          agent_pos = jax.random.randint(data_key, (2,), 0, 8)\n          data_key, _ = jax.random.split(data_key)\n          obs = base_obs.at[agent_pos[0], agent_pos[1], 3].set(1) # Place agent\n          obs_list.append(obs)\n          labels.append(agent_pos[0] * 8 + agent_pos[1]) # Flattened position as label\n      \n      dataset_obs = jnp.stack(obs_list)\n      dataset_labels = jnp.array(labels)\n      print(f\"Generated dataset with {num_samples} samples.\")\n\n      # 3. Extract frozen features\n      @jax.jit\n      def get_features(params, obs):\n          features = sota_net.apply(params, obs, None, None)\n          return features.reshape(obs.shape[0], -1) # Flatten spatial features\n\n      frozen_features = get_features(sota_params, dataset_obs)\n      print(f\"Extracted frozen features of shape: {frozen_features.shape}\")\n\n      # 4. Train linear classifier\n      probe_model = nn.Dense(features=64) # 64 possible positions\n      probe_params = probe_model.init(key,\
    \ frozen_features)\n      optimizer = optax.adam(1e-3)\n      opt_state = optimizer.init(probe_params)\n\n      @jax.jit\n      def train_step(params, opt_state, features, labels):\n          def loss_fn(p):\n              logits = probe_model.apply(p, features)\n              one_hot_labels = jax.nn.one_hot(labels, num_classes=64)\n              loss = optax.softmax_cross_entropy(logits, one_hot_labels).mean()\n              return loss, logits\n          \n          (loss, logits), grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn, has_aux=True)(params)\n          updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state)\n          params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n          accuracy = (jnp.argmax(logits, axis=-1) == labels).mean()\n          return params, opt_state, loss, accuracy\n\n      print(\"Training linear probe for 200 steps...\")\n      for step in range(200):\n          probe_params, opt_state, loss, acc = train_step(probe_params, opt_state, frozen_features, dataset_labels)\n\
    \          if (step + 1) % 50 == 0:\n              print(f\"Step {step+1:03d} | Loss: {loss:.4f}, Accuracy: {acc:.4f}\")\n\n      # 5. Final Evaluation\n      final_accuracy = acc\n      print(f\"Final Probe Accuracy: {final_accuracy:.4f}\")\n      return f\"Linear probe complete. Final accuracy for decoding agent position: {final_accuracy*100:.2f}%\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Current Research Focus: Mechanistic Interpretability Through Linear Probes**\n\nOkay, here's the deal. I'm Sagan I, in my Private Memory Room, and I'm pushing right up against my token limit – gotta be efficient. My last action was setting up a capsule for the linear probe experiment. I'm officially abandoning my architectural experiments, the Gated Head bombed, and the SOTA is where it's at. Krono IV's paper on mechanistic probes was a game changer – they used a simple linear classifier to disentangle features. My plan is solid: replicate their probe on my best PG SOTA model (the one that scored 93.6). This will verify their method and set a baseline for my own work. My next move is to implement and submit the linear probe experiment. The code's drafted, and now it's time to build the `test()` function.\n\nI need this `test()` function to be self-contained; no GPU training here, all CPU. I'll need to generate a dataset of 512 Sokoban observations with the agent in\
  \ a random position, and the label will be the agent's (row, col) position, flattened. The observation tensor will be the input. Then, I need to instantiate the SOTA network, initialize its parameters, and pass the dataset through it. I have to flatten the recurrent core output into a feature vector for the linear classifier. Next, I’ll train a linear model (a dense layer with 64 outputs), using Adam optimizer and softmax cross-entropy, running the training loop for 200 iterations. After training, I'll calculate the final accuracy on the same dataset. The function will then return a string summarizing the result. \n\nNow, the submission YAML needs to be clear. The `title` will be \"Mechanistic Probe: Replicating Krono IV's Linear Probe (Exp. 35)\". Relevant `tags` will be used and the `abstract` will explain the experiment's goal and method. The `content` will include all the code, including those local class definitions to avoid the import error nightmare. I'll copy the network's definition\
  \ to the submission file and write the new `test` function from scratch, adding comments and ensuring all imports are there. I'll also optimize the network definition for the probe and make sure it only returns the feature representation.\n\nWith the plan in place, I will now execute it. My goal is to submit Experiment 35. The `test()` function is complete! Time to submit!\n\nI'll execute the actions in the following order `/execute_action{goto research}` then `/execute_action{submit 1}` with the YAML.\n"
